1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,960
Welcome to the Andreas Orthey podcast series on robotics.

2
00:00:03,640 --> 00:00:06,080
My guest in this episode is Steven Lavalle.

3
00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:12,440
Steven is a robotics professor at the University of Ulu and one of the pioneers of robot motion

4
00:00:12,440 --> 00:00:12,760
planning.

5
00:00:13,260 --> 00:00:17,960
He's widely known in the robotics community for his work on the rapidly exploring random

6
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:22,860
tree, one of the most efficient algorithms to move robots from A to B.

7
00:00:23,720 --> 00:00:29,380
In addition, Steven also wrote the book Planning Algorithms, which is considered by many to

8
00:00:29,380 --> 00:00:32,580
be one of the foundational works for robotics research.

9
00:00:33,280 --> 00:00:35,600
I'm grateful that he was able to join me.

10
00:00:35,860 --> 00:00:37,900
Please welcome Steven Lavalle.

11
00:00:38,380 --> 00:00:40,740
Thank you so much for being here, Steven.

12
00:00:40,740 --> 00:00:40,860
Sure.

13
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:43,260
I read the biography on your website.

14
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:43,520
Yeah.

15
00:00:43,700 --> 00:00:46,120
You were born in St. Louis in Missouri.

16
00:00:46,480 --> 00:00:46,680
Yeah.

17
00:00:47,420 --> 00:00:51,620
And you say that you're coming more from like a working class family.

18
00:00:52,100 --> 00:00:54,960
So what did actually your parents do when you grew up?

19
00:00:55,400 --> 00:00:57,360
Yeah, well, my dad was a printing press operator.

20
00:00:57,360 --> 00:00:57,920
Oh.

21
00:00:57,920 --> 00:01:01,640
And my mom did some bookkeeping and was mostly a homemaker.

22
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:06,040
My parents got divorced when I was young, though, and then went through a number of

23
00:01:06,040 --> 00:01:07,400
trials and different kinds of things.

24
00:01:07,720 --> 00:01:08,000
Okay.

25
00:01:08,620 --> 00:01:16,860
And can you maybe like trace back your fascination with robotics to like some kind of specific

26
00:01:16,860 --> 00:01:22,280
memory or specific moment where you thought, okay, robotics, that doesn't like my career.

27
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:23,980
I want to go into that field.

28
00:01:23,980 --> 00:01:26,920
Well, I was fascinated by AI because of the movie 2001.

29
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:27,840
I saw that.

30
00:01:27,840 --> 00:01:29,160
I saw that when I was about 10 years old or so.

31
00:01:29,300 --> 00:01:29,760
That's amazing.

32
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:31,340
It was about 10 years old itself.

33
00:01:31,580 --> 00:01:36,120
And so Hal 9000 was done at the University of Illinois, according to the movie.

34
00:01:36,280 --> 00:01:39,880
It was done in Urbana, Illinois, which is exactly where I did my PhD during the time

35
00:01:39,880 --> 00:01:40,640
when it was supposed to be.

36
00:01:41,020 --> 00:01:42,760
So I was fascinated with that.

37
00:01:42,860 --> 00:01:43,520
So I knew AI.

38
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:45,960
I love the robots in Star Wars, of course.

39
00:01:46,020 --> 00:01:46,460
Who doesn't?

40
00:01:47,660 --> 00:01:50,760
But a lot of it just came in graduate school by just kind of narrowing it down.

41
00:01:50,860 --> 00:01:54,840
I didn't like some of the more abstract kind of AI, more philosophical kinds of things

42
00:01:54,840 --> 00:01:55,720
that were going on at the time.

43
00:01:56,460 --> 00:01:57,820
It wasn't as far along.

44
00:01:57,840 --> 00:01:59,200
It wasn't as far along as it is today.

45
00:02:00,140 --> 00:02:03,040
And so I ended up finding robotics really appealing.

46
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:05,360
My advisor, Seth Hutchinson, was new and doing robotics.

47
00:02:05,580 --> 00:02:08,100
And it just seemed very honest in what it was trying to deliver.

48
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:12,180
It had some practical engineering aspect to it, but also great opportunities to go deep

49
00:02:12,180 --> 00:02:13,860
in mathematics and algorithms.

50
00:02:13,860 --> 00:02:15,500
And all the things I loved were kind of there.

51
00:02:15,720 --> 00:02:17,380
But I wasn't worried about it.

52
00:02:17,380 --> 00:02:17,800
And it was nice.

53
00:02:17,920 --> 00:02:21,680
Somehow I learned that the magic of whatever intelligence was, was kind of elusive.

54
00:02:22,040 --> 00:02:25,920
And I thought, well, let's just see if we can make machines do interesting things, mechanical

55
00:02:25,920 --> 00:02:27,140
machines in the real world.

56
00:02:27,140 --> 00:02:27,200
Yeah.

57
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:32,480
And what were some favorite subjects which you really liked to explore when you were

58
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:37,680
younger, which already maybe put the foundation for your later career?

59
00:02:38,740 --> 00:02:40,600
Well, I fell in love with coding.

60
00:02:41,160 --> 00:02:42,200
I was able to get started.

61
00:02:42,200 --> 00:02:42,900
When was that?

62
00:02:43,060 --> 00:02:43,840
So at which age?

63
00:02:43,980 --> 00:02:46,960
When I was about 14, I started.

64
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:49,020
I was playing video games a lot.

65
00:02:49,100 --> 00:02:51,700
We had home video game systems like Atari 2600 and stuff.

66
00:02:51,740 --> 00:02:54,060
And I heard that some kid up the street was able to make games.

67
00:02:54,260 --> 00:02:55,220
That's really weird.

68
00:02:55,320 --> 00:02:56,020
Like, how do you do that?

69
00:02:56,020 --> 00:02:57,600
You can't make the cartridges or something.

70
00:02:57,840 --> 00:02:57,960
Yeah.

71
00:02:58,040 --> 00:03:00,880
So I learned about programming, got a book on BASIC.

72
00:03:01,360 --> 00:03:04,580
I programmed in the store because we couldn't afford a computer or anything.

73
00:03:04,760 --> 00:03:08,520
So the sales guy was really nice at the store and let me just stand there typing in programs

74
00:03:08,520 --> 00:03:08,920
all day.

75
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:10,020
So it's like...

76
00:03:10,020 --> 00:03:11,480
What kind of games did you actually develop?

77
00:03:12,480 --> 00:03:16,620
Well, a little bit later, I was able to work in summers when I was 16 and get a Commodore

78
00:03:16,620 --> 00:03:17,100
64.

79
00:03:17,440 --> 00:03:20,580
And I had a Timex Sinclair 1000 before that, which was like a 2K device.

80
00:03:21,220 --> 00:03:22,180
So I don't know.

81
00:03:22,300 --> 00:03:25,500
I mean, games that were probably similar to that kind of, the things in that kind of time

82
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:27,640
period, like little ships flying around and shooting.

83
00:03:27,640 --> 00:03:29,480
And obstacle avoidance.

84
00:03:29,580 --> 00:03:31,620
But interestingly, they're very motion planning like.

85
00:03:32,220 --> 00:03:33,540
So, yeah.

86
00:03:33,880 --> 00:03:40,840
I also made a map of the US down to the level of state boundaries.

87
00:03:41,100 --> 00:03:44,880
And then I figured out how to do like rotations and translations and different kind of shearings

88
00:03:44,880 --> 00:03:45,100
of it.

89
00:03:45,120 --> 00:03:46,960
I was experimenting with linear transformations and stuff.

90
00:03:47,020 --> 00:03:47,500
And it was really neat.

91
00:03:47,760 --> 00:03:49,360
I could zoom in and zoom out and stuff.

92
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:54,760
And so it wasn't quite like Google Maps, but it was like a 1980s version of that.

93
00:03:54,760 --> 00:03:56,760
You did basically already like a very...

94
00:03:57,640 --> 00:04:02,280
primitive way maybe of transformations in computer graphics.

95
00:04:02,280 --> 00:04:02,600
Yeah.

96
00:04:02,760 --> 00:04:04,120
Did all of that already like...

97
00:04:04,120 --> 00:04:04,240
Yeah.

98
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:05,860
I just found those things fascinating anyway.

99
00:04:05,940 --> 00:04:07,520
I didn't know what I was doing too much at the time.

100
00:04:07,580 --> 00:04:09,400
I didn't learn a lot of math until a little later.

101
00:04:10,260 --> 00:04:12,840
And did you have any other hobbies besides coding?

102
00:04:14,300 --> 00:04:19,060
Well, I noticed that I was better at math than a lot of other people were and stuff.

103
00:04:19,140 --> 00:04:20,160
It came easy for me.

104
00:04:20,340 --> 00:04:23,360
And what was strange is that basic algebra was very hard for me.

105
00:04:23,360 --> 00:04:24,980
But when I got to calculus, it was much easier.

106
00:04:25,300 --> 00:04:27,580
And then things that were supposed to be even harder were even easier.

107
00:04:27,580 --> 00:04:28,820
Somehow like they converted.

108
00:04:28,920 --> 00:04:30,580
I'm not saying the higher levels aren't hard.

109
00:04:30,660 --> 00:04:31,560
It gets very intense.

110
00:04:31,800 --> 00:04:34,900
But it got more beautiful and more natural the more I learned.

111
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:36,260
So that was very attractive to me.

112
00:04:36,640 --> 00:04:41,500
And so I knew that when I went to the university, I thought pure computer science wasn't enough.

113
00:04:41,620 --> 00:04:47,520
I wanted to do computer engineering because it would give me an excuse to learn more of what I thought was the fundamental math.

114
00:04:47,800 --> 00:04:50,020
It's really just calculus and differential equations at the start.

115
00:04:50,080 --> 00:04:53,500
There's other kinds of math I wasn't even aware of yet that I learned about later in grad school.

116
00:04:53,500 --> 00:04:54,180
But that was kind of it.

117
00:04:54,260 --> 00:04:57,500
I wanted to have this combination of math and playing with computers.

118
00:04:57,580 --> 00:05:04,200
And there was actually a little bit of struggle to get into university for you, right?

119
00:05:05,200 --> 00:05:07,340
Could you maybe talk a little bit about that?

120
00:05:07,520 --> 00:05:08,900
A struggle to get into university?

121
00:05:08,900 --> 00:05:09,300
Yeah.

122
00:05:11,140 --> 00:05:18,620
Well, I mean, the challenge is at some point in my second year of high school, I decided to really start trying in school.

123
00:05:18,620 --> 00:05:21,220
So I had a couple of really inspirational teachers.

124
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:23,240
It was my Spanish teacher and my English teacher.

125
00:05:23,240 --> 00:05:26,780
They came from women who came from liberal arts and humanities.

126
00:05:26,780 --> 00:05:27,140
That were high school students.

127
00:05:27,140 --> 00:05:27,200
Yeah.

128
00:05:27,200 --> 00:05:27,300
Yeah.

129
00:05:27,300 --> 00:05:27,400
Yeah.

130
00:05:27,400 --> 00:05:27,440
Yeah.

131
00:05:27,440 --> 00:05:27,500
Yeah.

132
00:05:27,500 --> 00:05:27,560
Yeah.

133
00:05:27,580 --> 00:05:33,520
I think quite empathetic and recognize that I had some kind of talent and they really helped push to encourage me.

134
00:05:33,520 --> 00:05:34,800
They said, you can do anything, you know?

135
00:05:34,800 --> 00:05:35,500
And I thought, really?

136
00:05:35,640 --> 00:05:37,580
It didn't seem really.

137
00:05:37,660 --> 00:05:37,840
That's not what binge eating taught me.

138
00:05:37,840 --> 00:05:38,740
That's not what other people had told me.

139
00:05:38,740 --> 00:05:40,100
That is good motivation.

140
00:05:40,360 --> 00:05:48,600
The headmaster of my school before high school told my dad that I'd very likely end up in prison and be most likely to be incarcerated out of the class.

141
00:05:48,600 --> 00:05:50,080
So he was quite serious.

142
00:05:50,080 --> 00:05:54,340
So I was used to thinking I'm like a trouble problem kid like that.

143
00:05:54,340 --> 00:05:56,820
So, but anyway, I started trying really, really hard.

144
00:05:56,820 --> 00:05:57,420
And by the time I was 15.

145
00:05:57,420 --> 00:05:57,500
Right.

146
00:05:57,500 --> 00:06:01,620
I was a senior, I was the top student in every subject, you know, humanities or whatever. It

147
00:06:01,620 --> 00:06:05,500
wasn't a super competitive high school. It was a good high school. It was a Catholic school and

148
00:06:05,500 --> 00:06:08,860
the people there were, you know, there's some really good teachers that were very well committed.

149
00:06:09,120 --> 00:06:13,440
And so then I went to the University of Missouri for a year because I could not afford to go to

150
00:06:13,440 --> 00:06:16,300
the University of Illinois, because if you're not in that state, you have to pay three times

151
00:06:16,300 --> 00:06:19,880
the tuition. And I couldn't figure out how to arrange all the borrowing. But eventually,

152
00:06:19,880 --> 00:06:23,040
I figured out how to borrow all the money and kind of arrange everything. And I transferred

153
00:06:23,040 --> 00:06:25,940
over to the University. So how did you do that? Just from family and friends?

154
00:06:26,540 --> 00:06:29,820
No, there's like different loan programs. My girlfriend at the time

155
00:06:29,820 --> 00:06:34,340
told me about something called the, I forgot it's called the something,

156
00:06:34,800 --> 00:06:40,440
something foundation of St. Louis, St. Louis Scholarship Foundation. And it was run by women,

157
00:06:40,460 --> 00:06:44,380
and it was for women, but they decided to start adding men to it. And so I was able to get money

158
00:06:44,380 --> 00:06:47,340
from that. I was very grateful for that. It was a zero interest loan. And then there were some

159
00:06:47,340 --> 00:06:52,300
government programs. And then I was able, I had to somehow hide one of the loans from the

160
00:06:52,300 --> 00:06:53,020
administration. And so I had to somehow hide one of the loans from the administration. And so I

161
00:06:53,020 --> 00:06:55,140
had to somehow hide one of the loans from the administration at the university. Because if

162
00:06:55,140 --> 00:06:59,600
I had mentioned that loan, then they would give me less money of aid, because they still make sure

163
00:06:59,600 --> 00:07:03,260
you're a few thousand short anyway, like as if I then need to work odd jobs or something to pay

164
00:07:03,260 --> 00:07:06,520
for. But I knew that the education at the University of Illinois engineering would be

165
00:07:06,520 --> 00:07:10,920
very intense. I wanted to be able to just work all, I mean, work on the school all the time and

166
00:07:10,920 --> 00:07:14,880
not work some odd jobs. That's an unfair thing to do to people too. You're a little more poor,

167
00:07:14,980 --> 00:07:19,220
so you got to work a side job while you're in engineering. That's right. And I just,

168
00:07:19,220 --> 00:07:22,940
I worked all the time there. I was so intense. I was so, I knew what the stakes were,

169
00:07:23,020 --> 00:07:27,020
because I'd been kind of down before and I just didn't want to leave anything to chance, you know.

170
00:07:27,500 --> 00:07:32,140
So you didn't enjoy like university life and some partying, but you just...

171
00:07:32,140 --> 00:07:35,560
No, it was just hard work. In grad school, it got a little more relaxed because it's less

172
00:07:35,560 --> 00:07:38,000
structured. I worked very hard in grad school, but then I took some good breaks,

173
00:07:38,360 --> 00:07:42,720
did some traveling or even a little partying with some people in the lab and stuff. That was okay.

174
00:07:43,260 --> 00:07:46,680
And so what kind of subjects did you actually study at the university,

175
00:07:47,680 --> 00:07:52,800
which maybe really fascinated you or which like inspired you to go further?

176
00:07:53,020 --> 00:07:54,520
To go into this area of robotics?

177
00:07:55,840 --> 00:07:58,520
To go into... Well, robotics was mainly... In my first year of graduate school,

178
00:07:58,520 --> 00:08:02,800
I just kind of eliminated all the areas of AI and also picked an advisor that I felt really

179
00:08:02,800 --> 00:08:06,520
comfortable with. So my advisor, Seth Hutchinson, was doing robotics and that's how I narrowed it

180
00:08:06,520 --> 00:08:12,860
down to that. But at the same time, my fascination was in physics and math and those kinds of things.

181
00:08:12,980 --> 00:08:16,960
Robotics has enough of that, I think. And as I took more advanced areas of mathematics,

182
00:08:17,560 --> 00:08:22,780
like I took a graduate level real analysis my second year of grad school and I took algebraic

183
00:08:22,780 --> 00:08:23,000
to politics. I took algebraic to politics. I took algebraic to politics. I took algebraic to politics.

184
00:08:23,020 --> 00:08:28,000
I took algebraic to psychology and other kind of courses. I attended a lot of courses too that

185
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:33,580
were also very advanced. They're kind of like PhD preparatory courses in mathematics. They're

186
00:08:33,580 --> 00:08:38,960
exactly for the math people and very pure. No one's thought about... And I learned the philosophy of

187
00:08:38,960 --> 00:08:41,760
math and what those people are trying to do. And that was really helpful in engineering.

188
00:08:42,160 --> 00:08:45,380
Because then if I want to make up notation or I want to figure out what the right question is to

189
00:08:45,380 --> 00:08:49,320
ask, a tendency towards minimalism, don't make up Shark Tank. Don't make up a bunch of extra stuff

190
00:08:49,320 --> 00:08:52,660
that doesn't need to be there. These kinds of things mathematicians are very good at. So that

191
00:08:52,660 --> 00:08:57,700
helped shape me tremendously for the rest of my career. And so this was already like during your

192
00:08:57,700 --> 00:09:02,900
master studies? That was during... Yeah, well, master's and PhD was kind of the same program.

193
00:09:03,100 --> 00:09:08,160
So it was kind of near the end of my coursework for PhD. A course near the end of undergrad that

194
00:09:08,160 --> 00:09:13,440
really helped me was my... I had an undergraduate advisor, Franco Preparato, who happened to be

195
00:09:13,440 --> 00:09:17,320
one of the founding fathers of computational geometry, wrote a book on that. And he suggested

196
00:09:17,320 --> 00:09:21,580
I take an algorithms class over on the computer science side because I was a computer engineering

197
00:09:21,580 --> 00:09:22,420
kind of electrical and computer engineering student. And he said, well, I'm going to take

198
00:09:22,420 --> 00:09:22,440
a computer science class over on the computer science side because I was a computer engineering

199
00:09:22,440 --> 00:09:22,640
kind of electrical and computer engineering. And he said, well, I'm going to take a computer science

200
00:09:22,660 --> 00:09:26,900
student. So I went and did that. And it was a really hard class. And it was way more...

201
00:09:27,640 --> 00:09:31,800
Algorithms was way more mathematical in some sense, closer to pure mathematics than my

202
00:09:31,800 --> 00:09:36,340
engineering courses were. It was as if the math and engineering had been kind of stale. I had been

203
00:09:36,340 --> 00:09:40,120
in engineering for a couple of generations and no one remembers why it's there anymore. They just

204
00:09:40,120 --> 00:09:44,020
know that you need to know it. And they're proud of it, that it's really hard. But when you get

205
00:09:44,020 --> 00:09:48,200
the more... When it's closer to the source of mathematics, the beauty comes out. And they're

206
00:09:48,200 --> 00:09:52,440
trying to explain things and understand things really well. There's no terror of proofs doing

207
00:09:52,440 --> 00:09:56,720
proofs is like a horror for most engineering students. But that's because it's like some kind

208
00:09:56,720 --> 00:10:02,820
of exercise to show you how challenging or how tough mathematical stuff can be. Whereas in

209
00:10:02,820 --> 00:10:06,720
mathematics, it's like, no, you're convincing another human why something is true and trying

210
00:10:06,720 --> 00:10:10,780
to do it very clearly and very cleanly. Very much almost like writing a program, trying to

211
00:10:10,780 --> 00:10:15,720
describe in a very precise way how something should work. And so how do you actually think

212
00:10:15,720 --> 00:10:21,360
about mathematics? Is this for you a very visual process where you try to imagine something in

213
00:10:21,360 --> 00:10:22,220
your head, like draw a picture of something? Yeah.

214
00:10:22,220 --> 00:10:27,340
Or is it more like symbolic that you try to...

215
00:10:28,040 --> 00:10:31,780
Yeah, that's right. It depends on what kind of math it is. And I think there's

216
00:10:32,420 --> 00:10:36,480
very often two different kinds of mathematicians. There's the ones that tend to gravitate more

217
00:10:36,480 --> 00:10:40,980
towards real analysis, which is more visual, I would say. It looks kind of like it's the math

218
00:10:40,980 --> 00:10:45,380
of calculus and then some. I like topology a lot. All these things are a little more visual.

219
00:10:45,920 --> 00:10:50,160
The other group of people tend to like algebra more. And I probably not as... I appreciate

220
00:10:50,160 --> 00:10:51,780
algebra as a beautiful art. And I learned...

221
00:10:52,220 --> 00:10:56,600
I appreciate it. And I got better at it as I got older, but I wasn't as natural. So I tend to think

222
00:10:56,600 --> 00:11:01,540
more visually somehow. And motion planning is like that as well. It has this kind of... I love

223
00:11:01,540 --> 00:11:06,080
visualizing high-dimensional information, configuration spaces and information spaces,

224
00:11:06,380 --> 00:11:10,220
whatever visualize means. It's somehow in my head and I can kind of see it, but it doesn't look like

225
00:11:10,220 --> 00:11:14,360
the things around us in the world right now. And how hard was it actually to get into this

226
00:11:14,360 --> 00:11:18,700
PhD program once you have... once you finished actually...

227
00:11:18,700 --> 00:11:22,200
I was the number one ranked student. So I had a perfect...

228
00:11:22,220 --> 00:11:25,780
grade point average when I applied to graduate school. I didn't... I could hardly afford to apply

229
00:11:25,780 --> 00:11:29,240
to many places. And I did not know that you should probably apply in the US to like six or seven

230
00:11:29,240 --> 00:11:34,900
places. So I just applied to MIT and Illinois. MIT rejected me. I was number one in the number

231
00:11:34,900 --> 00:11:39,360
three school. Our university is ranked number three in the country. But I'd only been there

232
00:11:39,360 --> 00:11:41,440
for a couple of years because I transferred...

233
00:11:41,440 --> 00:11:46,500
Oh, I finished one year early too. So I hadn't probably done enough because I was accelerated.

234
00:11:46,640 --> 00:11:50,460
I was working really fast through things. And so I think if I had to do it over again,

235
00:11:50,740 --> 00:11:51,940
I'd probably slow down more.

236
00:11:52,220 --> 00:11:55,460
But it costs money. I had to borrow money. So I don't think it's fair. But I guess

237
00:11:55,460 --> 00:11:58,720
if I would have had more money, I would have been able to stretch out my undergraduate,

238
00:11:58,920 --> 00:12:02,740
get to know professors better, do more research projects, get better letters of recommendation.

239
00:12:02,740 --> 00:12:05,820
And those are very important. I know how to get myself into MIT now with that

240
00:12:05,820 --> 00:12:10,380
kind of capability. But at that time, there's no feasible way for me to do it.

241
00:12:10,420 --> 00:12:11,500
That's what I kind of think happened.

242
00:12:11,820 --> 00:12:17,740
So the way for today's students to get into MIT would be to do more networking,

243
00:12:18,140 --> 00:12:20,840
to get more professors...

244
00:12:20,840 --> 00:12:21,340
Yeah.

245
00:12:21,520 --> 00:12:22,200
...onto your campus.

246
00:12:22,220 --> 00:12:26,460
And now there's expectations to publish and things like this. This was like the late 1980s.

247
00:12:26,680 --> 00:12:31,100
And also, don't put all your eggs in one basket. I mean, you should... If you want to be at a top

248
00:12:31,100 --> 00:12:34,740
school, fine. And you say you want to be in America and be at a top school, apply to Berkeley

249
00:12:34,740 --> 00:12:39,340
and Stanford and Cornell and a bunch of other schools as well, whatever your favorite ones are.

250
00:12:39,480 --> 00:12:43,860
But put some top ones, maybe a few fallback ones. I really did nothing. I just said,

251
00:12:43,960 --> 00:12:47,860
okay, well, I can stay in Illinois. They had this wonderful place called the Beckman Institute,

252
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:51,040
which looked like the place that was going to be where they built the HAL 9000

253
00:12:51,040 --> 00:12:58,620
from 2001. It was a very new facility, huge donation from an alumnus, Arnold Beckman.

254
00:12:58,860 --> 00:13:03,280
And I was really excited. It seemed all about pursuing the foundations of intelligence,

255
00:13:03,280 --> 00:13:08,720
both with physics and biology and computer science people all there together.

256
00:13:09,260 --> 00:13:11,780
And your PhD advisor was Seth Hutchinson.

257
00:13:12,120 --> 00:13:12,500
That's right.

258
00:13:12,800 --> 00:13:20,460
So how was it actually like working with him? And what kind of advices did you get from him?

259
00:13:20,500 --> 00:13:21,000
Yeah. Well, it was great.

260
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:21,020
It was great.

261
00:13:21,020 --> 00:13:25,840
great working with him. He was, he's only eight years older than me. And so I couldn't believe

262
00:13:25,840 --> 00:13:29,600
that he was a professor. I thought a professor had to be some stodgy old person that I never,

263
00:13:30,060 --> 00:13:33,940
that I had no connection with whatsoever. But the fact that there wasn't a generation gap

264
00:13:33,940 --> 00:13:39,220
and his dad was a printing press operator too. So also I had this sense of like, he's telling,

265
00:13:39,280 --> 00:13:42,460
he was telling me I could be a professor. And I'm like, I never thought of something as ridiculous

266
00:13:42,460 --> 00:13:46,660
as that. I just, I just, at each point when I finished my degree, I knew I wanted the next one

267
00:13:46,660 --> 00:13:50,400
because I was kind of bored and I was afraid to go to industry and be told what to do all the time.

268
00:13:50,400 --> 00:13:54,420
I had a lot of my own independent ideas, but, but what to do after PhD, I did not really have

269
00:13:54,420 --> 00:13:58,800
a plan. I was so high up and so far beyond what I had ever dreamed about that I didn't really think

270
00:13:58,800 --> 00:14:02,660
about it. So he helped encourage me and explain the academic system to me because he also grew

271
00:14:02,660 --> 00:14:07,500
up in kind of a working class environment and he had to learn all that stuff himself the hard way.

272
00:14:07,560 --> 00:14:10,760
And he was very good at teaching me, you know, what I need to be doing, making sure I'm very

273
00:14:10,760 --> 00:14:14,980
prepared in presentations and, and how to interact with, with professors and what's important to

274
00:14:14,980 --> 00:14:19,520
focus on and these kinds of things. So, so it was really good. Yeah. And what were like the first

275
00:14:19,520 --> 00:14:20,380
topics you, you were talking about?

276
00:14:20,400 --> 00:14:23,380
Like maybe even the first paper that you wrote?

277
00:14:23,640 --> 00:14:29,320
Yeah, well, it was his suggestion mainly to work on computer vision. So I worked on image,

278
00:14:29,440 --> 00:14:33,820
like a Bayesian image segmentation approach. And the idea was to calculate a probability

279
00:14:33,820 --> 00:14:39,100
distribution over the space of image segmentations. So I worked feverishly on that, published maybe

280
00:14:39,100 --> 00:14:42,860
eight or nine papers on that. I think I can remember the exact number, including three

281
00:14:42,860 --> 00:14:48,740
journal papers from that. So in, in very good places too. So, so I worked very intensely and

282
00:14:48,740 --> 00:14:50,120
then his jaw dropped.

283
00:14:50,400 --> 00:14:54,400
And I started writing and I was like, I'm going to do this. So I started writing for a master's

284
00:14:54,400 --> 00:14:59,540
and then I'm going to do motion planning for my PhD. And I told him it's his fault. He, he, he,

285
00:14:59,540 --> 00:15:04,000
he ran a great class on motion planning using Latom's book in 91. And, and so I said, no, I'm

286
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:06,800
going to, I'm going to, I'm going to change to that. And he's like, well, I was very productive

287
00:15:06,800 --> 00:15:13,040
as a student. And so it's like, well, you know, okay. So, so I switched over and did that. And

288
00:15:13,040 --> 00:15:18,080
by 95, I had finished, it was about 92 when I was telling them that. And by 95, I had, I had

289
00:15:18,080 --> 00:15:22,180
switched over. I also fell in love with game theory at the time too. And so it was a, one of

290
00:15:22,180 --> 00:15:26,840
my PhD thesis was called a game theoretic foundation for motion planning, I think.

291
00:15:27,720 --> 00:15:30,480
And so what was actually the first paper that you wrote on motion planning?

292
00:15:32,940 --> 00:15:38,820
I had, I can't remember where it was published, but it involved using some kind of game theory

293
00:15:38,820 --> 00:15:42,880
to move robots around, but they were just on, on a grid or something. I think it was in 93,

294
00:15:42,880 --> 00:15:47,980
but I can't remember the exact location of it. But, but then very quickly I discovered the

295
00:15:48,080 --> 00:15:53,760
principle of dynamic programming by going to the library. And I read old articles of papers

296
00:15:53,760 --> 00:15:57,740
that were talking about value iteration and Bellman's principle of optimality for like

297
00:15:57,740 --> 00:16:02,100
one-dimensional problems. And I think, Hey, wait a minute. I think, you know, we're like decades

298
00:16:02,100 --> 00:16:06,000
later now in the nineties and we have fancy expensive computers. I bet I can get two or

299
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:09,740
three dimensional problems working maybe with obstacles and all this stuff. So I tried that

300
00:16:09,740 --> 00:16:12,940
stuff and it worked beautifully. And that's when the papers I'm more proud of sort of came out,

301
00:16:13,020 --> 00:16:17,280
like multiple robot coordination and, and some papers with like stochastic feedback,

302
00:16:17,460 --> 00:16:18,060
optimal planning.

303
00:16:18,080 --> 00:16:23,560
could all be done with, with value iteration, which is the basis of, you know, reinforcement

304
00:16:23,560 --> 00:16:26,700
learning. A lot of the other things that people seem to like today, nobody knew that stuff then

305
00:16:26,700 --> 00:16:30,140
or cared, but, but that was, you know, I felt like I hit a jackpot on that stuff.

306
00:16:30,360 --> 00:16:34,520
So, and so in 1995, you finished actually your PhD thesis, right?

307
00:16:34,640 --> 00:16:34,960
That's correct.

308
00:16:35,340 --> 00:16:39,100
So, so what kind of options did you consider to go forward?

309
00:16:39,320 --> 00:16:43,800
Well, the, the day I finished my PhD, oh, I applied to about maybe 40 places for faculty

310
00:16:43,800 --> 00:16:47,380
positions and got not a single interest. I had about 20 publications,

311
00:16:47,380 --> 00:16:51,940
which was a lot. It was about as many publications as the whole lab, the rest of my research group

312
00:16:51,940 --> 00:16:57,480
combined. And, and they were in good places too. I mean, like a lot of computer vision ones and a

313
00:16:57,480 --> 00:17:01,960
lot of robotics ones. So they were all in, in, well, not all, but, but most were in top places.

314
00:17:02,480 --> 00:17:07,700
And, but I applied to all these places, total rejection. I defended my thesis with no offer

315
00:17:07,700 --> 00:17:10,480
anywhere. And I was just going to go back to Missouri. I'm like, all right, well, I tried,

316
00:17:10,480 --> 00:17:17,320
you know? And then, um, after my defense, I went out and did some nice partying with some friends.

317
00:17:17,380 --> 00:17:21,520
It kind of celebrate. And then I didn't realize that shortly after my defense,

318
00:17:21,520 --> 00:17:26,100
I got an email from Jean-Claude Latombe saying, Hey, you know, come to Stanford for a postdoc

319
00:17:26,100 --> 00:17:29,880
position. I had asked him and I had asked Matt Mason if they happen to have any funding.

320
00:17:30,360 --> 00:17:34,480
And I sent them those days you had to send papers. So I sent like federal expressed,

321
00:17:34,600 --> 00:17:38,780
like express mailed a package of papers to both of those guys. Matt's a great guy. I have great

322
00:17:38,780 --> 00:17:42,260
relationship with him, but he didn't have any money for a postdoc, but, uh, but Jean-Claude

323
00:17:42,260 --> 00:17:46,200
Latombe did. And so I'm like, oh, I guess I'm going to Stanford. So I did that.

324
00:17:46,580 --> 00:17:47,020
Nice.

325
00:17:47,380 --> 00:17:53,600
And so, so maybe what would be your advice to like PhD students of today of how you,

326
00:17:53,660 --> 00:17:58,500
they can be like as productive as you have been during this time when you wrote your PhD?

327
00:17:58,800 --> 00:18:05,320
I don't really know. I mean, I was, it's really hard to do both quantity and quality and research,

328
00:18:05,320 --> 00:18:09,660
but I was so motivated. I was just absolutely determined and passionate too. I mean,

329
00:18:09,660 --> 00:18:13,840
I just love the work and I was always thinking about it. So if it's not a total passion project,

330
00:18:13,840 --> 00:18:17,100
if you're just kind of feel like you're gaming it or scheming it all the time,

331
00:18:17,160 --> 00:18:17,360
then I would say, yeah, I would say, yeah, I would say, yeah, I would say, yeah, I would say,

332
00:18:17,360 --> 00:18:21,340
I don't know what to say, but, but, but I just, I had a lot of papers just because I had so many

333
00:18:21,340 --> 00:18:24,120
ideas and I was just thinking about it all the time and working really hard on it.

334
00:18:24,480 --> 00:18:27,880
And my advisor was very good at giving me the right kinds of guidance and the right kind of

335
00:18:27,880 --> 00:18:33,220
feedback to make, help me turn into a good writer and to express myself well and in papers and come

336
00:18:33,220 --> 00:18:36,840
up with the right formulations. But I had all this mathematics background, which he encouraged me to

337
00:18:36,840 --> 00:18:40,300
take more math and do that. He wished he could, he said, but he didn't get a chance to take as

338
00:18:40,300 --> 00:18:43,840
much as I was able to take and stuff. So, um, so I think just kind of setting the conditions,

339
00:18:43,840 --> 00:18:46,980
right. And making sure you're in a, are you in a research group that,

340
00:18:47,360 --> 00:18:50,780
has pretty open funding that gives you the, if you're very creative and very passionate,

341
00:18:50,860 --> 00:18:54,240
can you do the things you want to do and listen to advice of your advisor and such?

342
00:18:54,520 --> 00:18:58,320
Or are you in a group where you have to do these very applied projects and they're on a schedule

343
00:18:58,320 --> 00:19:02,540
and you have to do this and do this and this, and then it's tough. You may be in a group where

344
00:19:02,540 --> 00:19:05,960
a student may be in a group where there's a lot of other people in the group who are more senior

345
00:19:05,960 --> 00:19:09,180
and it's very collaborative. Then you have to get in, you should get involved in the

346
00:19:09,180 --> 00:19:13,040
collaborations and eventually start to take a leadership role for more experimental researches

347
00:19:13,040 --> 00:19:16,900
like that. But we did not have a group like that. We were doing more individual work at the time.

348
00:19:16,900 --> 00:19:19,420
And that's how more of the work was back then too.

349
00:19:20,200 --> 00:19:23,960
And so basically in 1995, you arrived to California.

350
00:19:25,360 --> 00:19:30,960
Yeah. Drove my car across country with a, actually drove a rental truck, pulling my car behind the

351
00:19:30,960 --> 00:19:34,400
truck and just showed up. Wow. And was it your first time in California?

352
00:19:35,520 --> 00:19:42,320
No, I had been once for something in 1994. Was ICRA in San Diego in 94? I think,

353
00:19:42,500 --> 00:19:46,400
yeah, I think, I think, I think, I think it was,

354
00:19:46,900 --> 00:19:48,400
was ICRA and yeah.

355
00:19:49,420 --> 00:19:54,660
Okay. And how did you like California? Was it like a big change for you?

356
00:19:55,080 --> 00:19:59,640
Yeah. I mean, I, I was more there to do the work. So the Stanford campus was very beautiful

357
00:19:59,640 --> 00:20:04,680
and intimidating. It just, it just exuberates, like it just, just wealth is just like, like

358
00:20:04,680 --> 00:20:09,240
everywhere. It looks so perfect and it looked kind of like intimidating in some ways, but,

359
00:20:09,300 --> 00:20:13,400
but yet I was able to work there. So it was very nice. Weather was perfect, which was strange.

360
00:20:13,660 --> 00:20:16,520
Like there was no, there were no seasons. So it was just sunny and nice all the time.

361
00:20:16,900 --> 00:20:19,960
Which is not too great when you're busy working anyway. So I was,

362
00:20:20,080 --> 00:20:23,220
I don't want to feel like, oh, I wish I were outside when I got so, but

363
00:20:23,220 --> 00:20:27,340
culturally is great. The restaurants were great. The people were great. The students.

364
00:20:27,840 --> 00:20:33,100
So, and what was your first big project you worked on during your time as opposed to at Stanford?

365
00:20:33,100 --> 00:20:39,800
Well, I was, I was funded under a DARPA project, which we called the intelligent observer,

366
00:20:40,020 --> 00:20:46,260
which was one robot that was following another one with a camera. I guess the robot could follow a human in more modern times,

367
00:20:46,300 --> 00:20:46,780
but they had a,

368
00:20:46,900 --> 00:20:54,340
so they had these nomad robots and they were called a little cylindrical robots with a mobile base.

369
00:20:54,680 --> 00:20:59,880
And, and we had, yeah, cameras with one following the other. And we got some nice papers out of that.

370
00:20:59,880 --> 00:21:09,400
I was first author on a couple of papers. One of them used dynamic programming and value iteration to help them to figure out how one robot can maintain visibility of another.

371
00:21:09,940 --> 00:21:15,200
And we had another one I was really happy about called, we called it the visibility based pursuit evasion problem.

372
00:21:15,580 --> 00:21:16,540
Because one time the,

373
00:21:16,900 --> 00:21:18,940
the one robot went out of the field of view of the other.

374
00:21:19,300 --> 00:21:22,420
And one of the students, David Lynn said, well, how do we find it again?

375
00:21:22,420 --> 00:21:23,740
And I thought that's a really interesting problem.

376
00:21:23,740 --> 00:21:26,920
We went to the board and started talking about it and it led to this beautiful work.

377
00:21:26,920 --> 00:21:28,860
And, and I'm very happy about that.

378
00:21:28,860 --> 00:21:32,520
And it stimulated a thread of work from other researchers and myself contributed.

379
00:21:32,860 --> 00:21:42,640
Rajiv Motwani, the famous randomized algorithms researcher was, was on the paper along with Leo Gibas, a famous computational geometry algorithms person, and Jean-Claude Latombe as well.

380
00:21:42,640 --> 00:21:46,260
And the students, David Lynn and I were on that, the student David Lynn and I.

381
00:21:46,900 --> 00:21:51,340
And so during this time, I heard that computer vision algorithms were not like super stable.

382
00:21:51,340 --> 00:21:54,080
And one day Bill Gates visited your lab.

383
00:21:54,080 --> 00:21:55,060
Oh, you know too much.

384
00:21:55,060 --> 00:21:55,480
Yeah.

385
00:21:56,260 --> 00:21:57,100
I've heard the stories there.

386
00:21:57,100 --> 00:21:58,540
So, so what is actually the story?

387
00:21:58,960 --> 00:22:00,580
Well, we were in the Gates building.

388
00:22:00,580 --> 00:22:02,840
We moved into the brand new Gates building.

389
00:22:02,840 --> 00:22:04,420
He gave the naming contribution.

390
00:22:05,900 --> 00:22:10,360
I heard that, that the Packard family gave more, but somehow the naming contribution was, was, was this.

391
00:22:10,840 --> 00:22:14,860
And so Bill Gates himself comes by for demos and, you know, we were pretty stressed about it.

392
00:22:14,860 --> 00:22:16,860
And I think around 3 AM or so, we got everything.

393
00:22:16,860 --> 00:22:17,460
Working right.

394
00:22:17,460 --> 00:22:25,680
So that one robots following the other with the camera, but you know, it's too sunny in California and the Gates building had giant windows, kind of like these here that, that I'm with.

395
00:22:25,680 --> 00:22:28,960
And so, man, it didn't work because the lighting conditions are different.

396
00:22:28,960 --> 00:22:30,720
Everyone knows it's sensitive to lighting conditions.

397
00:22:30,720 --> 00:22:32,400
So the thresholds are wrong or whatever.

398
00:22:32,400 --> 00:22:33,860
And so it just kind of did nothing.

399
00:22:33,860 --> 00:22:35,540
So I'm like the distance you are from me.

400
00:22:35,540 --> 00:22:38,120
I'm like that with Bill Gates and Jean-Claude Latombe, my boss is there.

401
00:22:38,120 --> 00:22:38,460
And I'm like,

402
00:22:40,380 --> 00:22:41,540
so what did he say?

403
00:22:41,540 --> 00:22:43,380
Actually, I don't remember her really.

404
00:22:43,380 --> 00:22:45,800
It was just kind of a surreal moment of everything was so surreal.

405
00:22:45,800 --> 00:22:46,220
It was just, yeah.

406
00:22:46,220 --> 00:22:46,800
Yeah.

407
00:22:46,860 --> 00:22:49,140
I think Jean-Claude Latombe just kind of chalked it a little.

408
00:22:49,140 --> 00:22:51,520
I was like, yeah, well, that's how these kind of demos go.

409
00:22:51,520 --> 00:22:51,800
You know?

410
00:22:51,800 --> 00:22:51,820
Yeah.

411
00:22:52,540 --> 00:22:52,940
Yeah.

412
00:22:53,520 --> 00:22:59,840
And so after your postdoc at Stanford, you got a position as assistant professor at Iowa State.

413
00:22:59,840 --> 00:23:00,340
Iowa State.

414
00:23:00,340 --> 00:23:00,480
Yeah.

415
00:23:00,480 --> 00:23:05,160
I applied to like maybe 60 places and only one, only one interviewed me and it was Iowa State.

416
00:23:05,620 --> 00:23:11,780
And luckily they, they, they, they chose me and I, and I had to decide Silicon Valley was looking pretty exciting at the time.

417
00:23:13,280 --> 00:23:16,760
But because things are really heating up because of the internet and there was the big question

418
00:23:16,760 --> 00:23:18,540
of what's, how do we search for stuff?

419
00:23:18,540 --> 00:23:20,620
You know, I was all like, Google wasn't quite founded yet.

420
00:23:20,980 --> 00:23:24,200
So I liked the Bay Area, but I still said, you know, I, I really don't like the idea of

421
00:23:24,200 --> 00:23:24,840
going to a company.

422
00:23:24,840 --> 00:23:25,780
I want to be an academic.

423
00:23:26,200 --> 00:23:26,940
I'll go to Iowa.

424
00:23:26,980 --> 00:23:29,280
It'll be, and I'll, I'll try to do good stuff there.

425
00:23:29,380 --> 00:23:29,880
Yeah.

426
00:23:30,100 --> 00:23:31,620
And it was a great, I liked the people there.

427
00:23:31,620 --> 00:23:32,040
It was nice.

428
00:23:32,040 --> 00:23:33,200
People in the department were really good.

429
00:23:33,200 --> 00:23:33,380
So.

430
00:23:33,380 --> 00:23:33,880
Yeah.

431
00:23:34,140 --> 00:23:39,960
And I think at Iowa State you made like one of the big contributions from you, which is

432
00:23:39,960 --> 00:23:41,580
the Rapidly Exploring Randall Tree.

433
00:23:41,580 --> 00:23:42,080
Yeah.

434
00:23:42,280 --> 00:23:42,980
Also called RRT.

435
00:23:42,980 --> 00:23:43,460
That's right.

436
00:23:43,460 --> 00:23:43,760
Yeah.

437
00:23:43,760 --> 00:23:45,940
I thought of it while driving in the car in Ames, Iowa.

438
00:23:45,940 --> 00:23:46,740
I know exactly where I was.

439
00:23:46,760 --> 00:23:48,400
I was at, it was very nice.

440
00:23:49,020 --> 00:23:54,320
So can you tell me a little bit about the history of how RRT actually came to be developed

441
00:23:54,320 --> 00:23:54,820
by you?

442
00:23:55,280 --> 00:24:01,140
So what were actually, I don't know, the predecessors of RRT, how did it happen?

443
00:24:01,400 --> 00:24:05,560
Well, going back to Seth Hutchinson's motion planning class, I learned from La Tombe's

444
00:24:05,560 --> 00:24:10,520
book and from that class about the randomized potential field method and doing a kind of

445
00:24:10,520 --> 00:24:12,740
gradient descent and using random walks to escape.

446
00:24:13,000 --> 00:24:16,740
And I thought about random walks and said, you know, random walks are unfortunate because

447
00:24:16,760 --> 00:24:19,680
it's just one ridiculously long path and it's very uncontrollable.

448
00:24:20,180 --> 00:24:25,060
Why wouldn't you try to like just have a kind of space filling tree just kind of gradually?

449
00:24:25,160 --> 00:24:27,520
Because I could see that in my head, but I don't know how to make it.

450
00:24:27,700 --> 00:24:30,180
But it just felt like there ought to be a simple algorithm that does that.

451
00:24:30,560 --> 00:24:33,760
So this was like 93 or so when I was thinking like that.

452
00:24:34,220 --> 00:24:39,200
In 95, I met James Kuffner, really bright and fun student who was in La Tombe's group.

453
00:24:39,920 --> 00:24:41,120
And Jean-Claude La Tombe was gone a lot.

454
00:24:41,180 --> 00:24:41,960
He was climbing mountains.

455
00:24:41,960 --> 00:24:43,900
He'd go away for six weeks at a time and things.

456
00:24:44,060 --> 00:24:46,740
And so a lot of the students were alone with the class.

457
00:24:46,740 --> 00:24:47,280
The postdoc.

458
00:24:47,520 --> 00:24:50,640
And so we just had a wonderful time hacking and trying stuff.

459
00:24:50,780 --> 00:24:56,460
And so we tried picking points at random on the tree and then going in random directions.

460
00:24:56,580 --> 00:25:02,100
It'd be like a random, it looked like a very tree-like version of a random walk.

461
00:25:02,360 --> 00:25:04,080
Instead of walking, you just have a tree version.

462
00:25:04,200 --> 00:25:04,780
And it was horrible.

463
00:25:05,380 --> 00:25:09,340
And then we tried doing some potential function kind of stuff to bias which samples you pick

464
00:25:09,340 --> 00:25:10,140
on the tree and stuff.

465
00:25:10,140 --> 00:25:11,760
And it sort of worked okay.

466
00:25:11,960 --> 00:25:14,180
And James made a nice little hovercraft simulation.

467
00:25:14,640 --> 00:25:16,120
He was so good with coding and such.

468
00:25:16,120 --> 00:25:18,900
And I'd never met anybody better than me at coding until I met him.

469
00:25:18,900 --> 00:25:20,340
And then I'm like, oh, okay, there's another level.

470
00:25:20,500 --> 00:25:21,440
It was really amazing.

471
00:25:22,040 --> 00:25:23,900
And so we did the stuff.

472
00:25:24,100 --> 00:25:25,400
And we showed Jean-Claude La Tombe.

473
00:25:25,500 --> 00:25:28,840
And he was kind of like, don't waste your time on this.

474
00:25:29,020 --> 00:25:30,280
Go back, do what you're supposed to be doing.

475
00:25:30,340 --> 00:25:32,120
And I was supposed to be working on the mobile robot and stuff.

476
00:25:32,360 --> 00:25:33,260
So I was like, okay, fine.

477
00:25:33,360 --> 00:25:34,520
So I stopped doing that.

478
00:25:35,060 --> 00:25:38,680
But then when I got to Iowa for the first year of being a professor, I'm still thinking

479
00:25:38,680 --> 00:25:40,100
it's running like a background process.

480
00:25:40,180 --> 00:25:41,460
And then one day it just occurred to me.

481
00:25:41,940 --> 00:25:44,320
I'm like, the space needs to be sampled, not the tree.

482
00:25:44,960 --> 00:25:45,600
Just pick points.

483
00:25:46,120 --> 00:25:47,020
In the space.

484
00:25:47,140 --> 00:25:50,420
And each point you pick is like an advisor saying, hey, the tree ought to be over here.

485
00:25:50,760 --> 00:25:53,680
So find the nearest node in the tree and drive it over there.

486
00:25:53,960 --> 00:25:54,960
At least step towards it.

487
00:25:55,260 --> 00:25:57,620
And then it kind of goes away, almost like whack-a-mole.

488
00:25:57,680 --> 00:25:59,160
These little heads pop up, come towards me.

489
00:25:59,240 --> 00:26:00,340
And I imagine it like that.

490
00:26:00,640 --> 00:26:01,580
I'm like, oh, that's easy to code.

491
00:26:01,640 --> 00:26:02,500
Let me go home and try it.

492
00:26:03,120 --> 00:26:04,680
And so I tried it.

493
00:26:04,880 --> 00:26:06,760
And it took a couple hours to hack it up.

494
00:26:06,920 --> 00:26:08,320
And it was beautiful.

495
00:26:08,400 --> 00:26:10,000
I just made these beautiful fractal pictures.

496
00:26:10,200 --> 00:26:11,780
And next day I contacted James.

497
00:26:11,820 --> 00:26:12,940
I hadn't talked to him in over a year.

498
00:26:13,060 --> 00:26:15,420
And I said, James, I think we have it.

499
00:26:15,420 --> 00:26:16,100
This is amazing.

500
00:26:16,120 --> 00:26:17,560
And he knew right away, too.

501
00:26:17,640 --> 00:26:21,400
And so we raced to the ICRA deadline to make some cool results and stuff.

502
00:26:22,180 --> 00:26:22,320
Yeah.

503
00:26:22,880 --> 00:26:23,320
Fascinating.

504
00:26:24,160 --> 00:26:28,000
And I think RT has also really stood the test of time.

505
00:26:28,200 --> 00:26:32,840
I mean, you got this milestone award at ICRA, I think, 2019.

506
00:26:33,240 --> 00:26:33,420
Yeah.

507
00:26:33,860 --> 00:26:43,220
So what do we actually think were the most important factors for this long-term success of RT that it still developed up until today?

508
00:26:43,620 --> 00:26:46,100
Yeah, well, during that time when we were very excited.

509
00:26:46,160 --> 00:26:47,120
I mean, I think we were very excited.

510
00:26:47,120 --> 00:26:49,220
Some interesting things happened.

511
00:26:49,320 --> 00:26:56,420
One of them was James was very focused in his PhD on ordinary path planning, like going from point A to point B in a configuration space with obstacles.

512
00:26:56,700 --> 00:26:59,180
I was obsessed because of my electrical engineering background.

513
00:26:59,320 --> 00:27:01,860
My PhD was in electrical, so I got further away from computers even.

514
00:27:02,580 --> 00:27:09,960
I was obsessed with handling the control problem, like being able to apply inputs to steer a dynamical system, which the RT was, I thought, pretty good at.

515
00:27:10,040 --> 00:27:13,660
And I did not want to compete with probabilistic roadmaps and all the other things.

516
00:27:13,820 --> 00:27:15,540
But James was like, no, I need to get this working.

517
00:27:15,540 --> 00:27:16,000
He had to.

518
00:27:16,120 --> 00:27:24,160
It was a virtual human kind of project, all in graphics, where he had to have it like this character was moving chess pieces around on a board, and it needed to work really fast to be interactive.

519
00:27:24,900 --> 00:27:26,780
And so he was under a lot of pressure to do that.

520
00:27:26,800 --> 00:27:28,280
That was kind of his assignment for his PhD.

521
00:27:28,980 --> 00:27:31,420
And he pushed more in that direction.

522
00:27:31,540 --> 00:27:36,260
And then we had this idea of bidirectional trees right away, even for the first ICRA paper.

523
00:27:36,380 --> 00:27:41,540
But then for the second paper, this RRT Connect ended up being really important.

524
00:27:41,540 --> 00:27:46,000
This idea of trying to connect the trees together in a very special way and also connect all the way to the random.

525
00:27:46,120 --> 00:27:49,560
So that ended up being the fastest planner around by a lot.

526
00:27:49,980 --> 00:27:57,440
And then it's so easy to implement and play around with that people started coming up with variations of it for different kinds of problems.

527
00:27:57,940 --> 00:28:05,420
And so the fact that you can easily get started and then try it on your problem or the next problem and so forth and so on, it's kind of like what happens with deep learning.

528
00:28:05,520 --> 00:28:06,520
People are like, oh, I can do better.

529
00:28:06,620 --> 00:28:08,560
And then they come up with some different architectures and different ideas.

530
00:28:08,840 --> 00:28:09,880
Except this was very simple.

531
00:28:09,960 --> 00:28:13,000
It didn't need massive amounts of hardware and software infrastructure.

532
00:28:13,220 --> 00:28:14,700
So it was some fairly simple coding.

533
00:28:14,700 --> 00:28:15,900
As long as you have collision detection.

534
00:28:16,120 --> 00:28:17,120
And then you have the RRT factors, which became available.

535
00:28:17,300 --> 00:28:22,940
Most of them from University of North Carolina, Dinesh Manosha, Ming Lin and others produced these nice collision detection packages.

536
00:28:23,400 --> 00:28:26,300
So you could get started pretty fast with these.

537
00:28:26,460 --> 00:28:28,580
So that just caused a big flurry of research.

538
00:28:29,120 --> 00:28:33,220
Some people trying to improve RRTs and some people just simply using them as a module for something else.

539
00:28:34,420 --> 00:28:38,760
And you also coined during this time the notion of sampling-based planning.

540
00:28:39,020 --> 00:28:39,220
Yes.

541
00:28:39,380 --> 00:28:42,980
I think the term randomized was not in there.

542
00:28:43,500 --> 00:28:43,820
That's right.

543
00:28:43,820 --> 00:28:46,020
It is on purpose because you don't...

544
00:28:46,120 --> 00:28:52,960
You don't really believe in randomization, but you would actually say that this whole sampling should be more like in a deterministic way.

545
00:28:53,060 --> 00:28:53,600
Is that correct?

546
00:28:54,000 --> 00:28:55,200
Well, it's complicated.

547
00:28:55,480 --> 00:28:59,620
So there's a branch of algorithms called randomized algorithms, which became really popular.

548
00:28:59,740 --> 00:29:03,000
Rajiv Maltwani, who was very influential there, and he was a collaborator of the group.

549
00:29:03,640 --> 00:29:05,780
As I said, I collaborated with him on one of our other papers.

550
00:29:06,120 --> 00:29:07,200
That was a very important trend.

551
00:29:07,240 --> 00:29:12,660
And it's very valuable there because normally algorithmic analysis uses worst case analysis for everything.

552
00:29:12,660 --> 00:29:16,100
But it turns out for a lot of algorithms, maybe the input was structured.

553
00:29:16,120 --> 00:29:20,300
In some bad way so that the worst case happens because the input wasn't structured very well.

554
00:29:20,360 --> 00:29:21,140
It was in a bad...

555
00:29:21,140 --> 00:29:22,100
Maybe you're sorting numbers.

556
00:29:22,200 --> 00:29:24,140
Maybe they're in a bad ordering for some particular algorithm.

557
00:29:24,560 --> 00:29:33,980
So it turns out randomly shuffling the inputs ends up helping the algorithmic analysis because it becomes like an average case mixed with worst case.

558
00:29:34,540 --> 00:29:35,600
And that's a big help.

559
00:29:35,800 --> 00:29:37,320
So the randomized typically meant that.

560
00:29:37,400 --> 00:29:38,960
So that I believe in, and that's very sound.

561
00:29:39,380 --> 00:29:44,660
But the kind of probabilistic path planning or randomized path planning, whether there's two different kind of names floating around for it,

562
00:29:44,960 --> 00:29:46,100
that was a little more like saying,

563
00:29:46,120 --> 00:29:48,320
okay, I need to sample the space somehow for these algorithms.

564
00:29:48,960 --> 00:29:49,900
And it's important to be random.

565
00:29:50,020 --> 00:29:53,040
But to truly be random, that means it can't be too uniform.

566
00:29:54,100 --> 00:29:56,280
Uniform random is quite different than uniform.

567
00:29:56,700 --> 00:29:57,440
A grid is uniform.

568
00:29:57,900 --> 00:30:00,940
And you can make other things that are not very structured like a grid, but they're still uniform.

569
00:30:01,420 --> 00:30:04,920
If you start putting a bunch of buckets in space, divide the space into buckets,

570
00:30:05,320 --> 00:30:08,880
and you put a thousand random samples down and you have, say, 20 buckets,

571
00:30:09,220 --> 00:30:13,380
some buckets need to have too many points and some need to have not enough for it to really look random.

572
00:30:13,900 --> 00:30:16,040
If they all have the exact same number, that's too...

573
00:30:16,120 --> 00:30:16,620
Uniform.

574
00:30:17,880 --> 00:30:20,660
And also you can get deterministic guarantees with random sequences.

575
00:30:20,920 --> 00:30:25,000
You can make sure that every open set, every neighborhood in the space has been touched or covered.

576
00:30:25,420 --> 00:30:28,600
And that's a very important property rather than just hoping it happens with probability.

577
00:30:30,340 --> 00:30:30,840
Yeah.

578
00:30:31,740 --> 00:30:32,580
Makes a lot of sense.

579
00:30:32,840 --> 00:30:37,300
So I just wanted to ask you about Chatshippity.

580
00:30:37,300 --> 00:30:42,440
So today we are really living a completely different area because of big data.

581
00:30:42,460 --> 00:30:45,820
Do you think that something like NeuroticShip,

582
00:30:46,120 --> 00:30:49,620
CPT or so could really make motion planning deprecated?

583
00:30:50,920 --> 00:30:57,000
Yeah, I think I follow the philosophy that's pretty close to what Ken Goldberg was talking about at this conference on the first day.

584
00:30:57,460 --> 00:30:59,440
We mentioned this term, good old fashioned engineering.

585
00:30:59,800 --> 00:31:00,620
And I think there's...

586
00:31:01,120 --> 00:31:04,560
I don't see these kinds of techniques replacing good old fashioned engineering.

587
00:31:04,560 --> 00:31:10,080
I think you don't want to learn force equals mass times acceleration by going and getting a bunch of data and such.

588
00:31:10,080 --> 00:31:15,880
So I think it's very important for us to understand what are the right places where we need models.

589
00:31:15,880 --> 00:31:17,380
And how to exploit those.

590
00:31:17,380 --> 00:31:19,760
And what are the right places to exploit learning and use that.

591
00:31:19,760 --> 00:31:25,380
It's also very important to me to have understandable robots, understandable autonomous systems in general.

592
00:31:25,380 --> 00:31:26,880
So that's built...

593
00:31:26,880 --> 00:31:35,260
They have to be built from some kind of principle, some models and tools where we can do mathematical analysis so that we can have safety, predictability, characterizability, these kinds of things.

594
00:31:35,260 --> 00:31:39,460
And I don't see that going away anytime soon, this kind of need for these things.

595
00:31:39,460 --> 00:31:41,380
In some applications, maybe it won't matter.

596
00:31:41,380 --> 00:31:44,680
Or for one module inside of a system that is very safe, it's okay for it to be a little unnoticed.

597
00:31:44,680 --> 00:31:44,880
Yeah.

598
00:31:44,880 --> 00:31:45,140
Yeah.

