622
00:31:47,080 --> 00:31:47,160
Yeah.

623
00:31:51,240 --> 00:31:52,720
And that's how it looks to me.

624
00:31:53,300 --> 00:31:58,100
And do you believe that there's still value in doing like fundamental motion planning research?

625
00:31:58,200 --> 00:31:59,700
So something like...

626
00:31:59,700 --> 00:32:01,640
Which could like replace RT, for example.

627
00:32:02,220 --> 00:32:08,880
Or do you think that this is really the best we could have ever come up with and there's not really value in it?

628
00:32:09,100 --> 00:32:10,420
Well, I hope it's the best.

629
00:32:10,560 --> 00:32:11,480
No, just kidding.

630
00:32:13,040 --> 00:32:15,780
Well, I think there's definitely a lot of room for fundamental...

631
00:32:15,780 --> 00:32:20,920
fundamental planning research. Planning research or computational control theory,

632
00:32:21,020 --> 00:32:23,760
it's almost the same kind of thing, largely. A lot of the problems are kind of the same.

633
00:32:24,160 --> 00:32:30,040
So there's a lot of openings for that. I think that the thing is,

634
00:32:31,120 --> 00:32:36,080
right now the planning has been, or up to now, the planning has been mainly on configuration space

635
00:32:36,080 --> 00:32:42,080
or an extended phase space. I'm very excited about information spaces, which is the space

636
00:32:42,080 --> 00:32:45,960
you naturally live in when you don't have perfect state information. And I think that's where the

637
00:32:45,960 --> 00:32:49,840
kind of wild frontier is and a lot of potential is there. Also, a lot of different types of feedback

638
00:32:49,840 --> 00:32:54,840
motion planning where it's not a path you're computing, but rather a policy. And there's

639
00:32:54,840 --> 00:32:57,900
lots of opportunities there as well. So I think there's a lot of room for movement in that.

640
00:32:58,560 --> 00:33:04,220
And there's always probably more room for mathematical analysis and better characterizations

641
00:33:04,220 --> 00:33:06,380
and these kinds of things. And I think those will improve.

642
00:33:07,440 --> 00:33:11,260
And do you believe that information spaces is something which could maybe replace

643
00:33:11,260 --> 00:33:12,060
configuration space?

644
00:33:12,080 --> 00:33:16,420
Information spaces, which have been so unifiedly used in robotics.

645
00:33:16,720 --> 00:33:20,040
Well, let's put it this way. If you take something like, imagine the notion of a digital twin,

646
00:33:20,180 --> 00:33:25,580
right? So there's an outside world and I want to capture all of it and put it inside the robot's

647
00:33:25,580 --> 00:33:30,200
brain and have a perfect sort of copy. So that's kind of the way the configuration space planning

648
00:33:30,200 --> 00:33:33,880
looks, right? Because in the robot's brain, it has a perfect model of all the geometry.

649
00:33:34,520 --> 00:33:38,180
So the information space is a replacement for the case where you don't want to do that,

650
00:33:38,440 --> 00:33:41,140
where you just say, okay, maybe my goal is to vacuum the floors.

651
00:33:41,400 --> 00:33:42,060
Do I need a configuration space?

652
00:33:42,060 --> 00:33:44,620
Do I need a complete map of the environment to do that with exact localization?

653
00:33:44,680 --> 00:33:50,100
Or maybe I can just kind of slop around or do some clever local strategies and just kind of make it work.

654
00:33:50,140 --> 00:33:54,420
Maybe there's some other ways to do it that do not require constructing a complete geometric map.

655
00:33:54,640 --> 00:33:57,980
It's like humans can do a lot of things without having complete precise maps and such.

656
00:33:58,600 --> 00:34:01,340
So that's what the information space is. It's like it is a replacement.

657
00:34:01,920 --> 00:34:08,260
But if you're going to do a digital twin, the information space just reduces to being the configuration space.

658
00:34:08,320 --> 00:34:10,060
So it's, in fact, a big generalization of that.

