685
00:34:12,840 --> 00:34:12,880
Yeah.

686
00:34:12,880 --> 00:34:12,900
Yeah.

687
00:34:12,900 --> 00:34:12,960
Yeah.

688
00:34:12,960 --> 00:34:13,180
Yeah.

689
00:34:13,180 --> 00:34:13,260
Yeah.

690
00:34:13,260 --> 00:34:13,320
Yeah.

691
00:34:13,320 --> 00:34:13,360
Yeah.

692
00:34:13,360 --> 00:34:13,420
Yeah.

693
00:34:13,420 --> 00:34:13,440
Yeah.

694
00:34:13,440 --> 00:34:13,860
Yeah.

695
00:34:13,860 --> 00:34:13,980
Yeah.

696
00:34:13,980 --> 00:34:14,040
Yeah.

697
00:34:14,040 --> 00:34:14,080
Yeah.

698
00:34:14,080 --> 00:34:14,100
Yeah.

699
00:34:14,100 --> 00:34:14,360
Yeah.

700
00:34:14,360 --> 00:34:15,620
surficient robot brain.

701
00:34:15,800 --> 00:34:18,320
So how does it actually tie in with that?

702
00:34:18,660 --> 00:34:24,280
So it basically says that the robot brain, I'm not reallychi talking about neurons and such,

703
00:34:24,280 --> 00:34:28,880
but let's imagine that it's just some very general discrete time or discrete event dynamical

704
00:34:28,880 --> 00:34:32,120
system, and it can be over a continuous state space or discrete state space.

705
00:34:32,500 --> 00:34:34,040
So it's a very general setting.

706
00:34:34,660 --> 00:34:39,480
And then we have sensors that measure information, measure whatever it is from the outside world.

707
00:34:39,540 --> 00:34:40,240
They get observations.

708
00:34:40,240 --> 00:34:42,120
And then there's actuation that goes out.

709
00:34:42,620 --> 00:34:46,780
And through this kind of interaction, the question is, if you express a task in the

710
00:34:46,780 --> 00:34:50,380
outside world, it could be in terms of you want to see these kind of sensor readings

711
00:34:50,380 --> 00:34:53,740
and you want to actuate these things, or maybe there's a state that you can reason about.

712
00:34:53,820 --> 00:34:54,360
It's external.

713
00:34:54,880 --> 00:34:59,960
In order to achieve these things, what's the smallest kind of like computation unit?

714
00:35:00,100 --> 00:35:01,980
We call it an information transition system.

715
00:35:02,360 --> 00:35:06,160
There's the internal states are information states just means internal states.

716
00:35:06,220 --> 00:35:08,060
There's no kind of meaning to the information necessarily.

717
00:35:08,060 --> 00:35:09,440
It may or may not have meaning to it.

718
00:35:09,440 --> 00:35:16,380
In a semantic sense, but basically, our theory says in a very general setting that there's

719
00:35:16,380 --> 00:35:22,840
always a smallest information space or information transition system for any given task.

720
00:35:23,160 --> 00:35:24,060
And it's unique.

721
00:35:24,440 --> 00:35:26,360
Now, finding it is very challenging.

722
00:35:26,460 --> 00:35:29,880
We found some before, and I think the search is on to find the rest of them.

723
00:35:29,960 --> 00:35:30,980
Can you find some automatically?

724
00:35:31,140 --> 00:35:31,400
Maybe.

725
00:35:32,220 --> 00:35:37,520
I think Jason O'Kane and Dylan Schell at Texas A&M, for example, are interested in this as well.

726
00:35:37,820 --> 00:35:39,420
Well, yeah.

727
00:35:39,440 --> 00:35:43,200
Let me talk a little bit about your book, Planning Algorithms.

728
00:35:43,200 --> 00:35:49,140
I think in 2004, you had a sabbatical and you just concentrated on writing this book.

729
00:35:49,620 --> 00:35:57,280
And I think it's a very special book because it's super comprehensive and there is such a big attention to detail in the book.

730
00:35:57,680 --> 00:36:03,000
So what did it actually take to write such a big monumental book?

731
00:36:03,220 --> 00:36:06,240
And also, how did you get inspired to do that?

732
00:36:06,600 --> 00:36:09,320
Yeah, well, I thought Jean-Claude de Tombe's book was really cool.

733
00:36:09,320 --> 00:36:09,400
Yeah.

734
00:36:09,440 --> 00:36:12,300
It was really cool in 1991, and it had some impact on me.

735
00:36:12,700 --> 00:36:15,680
I could see the respect that he got and the impact he was able to have.

736
00:36:15,840 --> 00:36:20,720
And in 1994 or so, when I was nearing the end of my PhD, I had a vision.

737
00:36:21,040 --> 00:36:23,500
I was writing a thesis, and I loved writing.

738
00:36:23,780 --> 00:36:27,820
And in pulling everything together for my thesis, I could see how to unify all kinds of things.

739
00:36:27,860 --> 00:36:29,900
In that case, it was a kind of game theoretic framework.

740
00:36:30,400 --> 00:36:31,780
And I was like, you know, I want to go bigger than this.

741
00:36:31,780 --> 00:36:39,060
I want to really try to unify control theory, discrete like AI kind of planning, and continuous motion planning, and kind of pull everything together.

742
00:36:39,440 --> 00:36:40,780
And I thought I could do a good job on that.

743
00:36:41,040 --> 00:36:49,840
So across the 90s, I was thinking about this all the time and just kind of building the landscape in my mind and doing research and kind of still putting the pieces together.

744
00:36:49,940 --> 00:36:56,140
So once I could kind of see the whole landscape in my head, in 2004, I'm like, okay, I'm ready to do a massive brain dump.

745
00:36:56,520 --> 00:37:03,280
And so I went to Poland, Poznań, and Professor Krzysztof Kozłowski was my host there.

746
00:37:03,940 --> 00:37:09,380
And I just mostly I couldn't use the university too much because they had a lot of rules about.

747
00:37:09,440 --> 00:37:11,960
When you could go to the office and they controlled the keys and stuff.

748
00:37:12,120 --> 00:37:19,400
So I eventually just got an apartment with high speed internet near the university and just kind of quarantined myself for the most part and just worked like crazy.

749
00:37:20,620 --> 00:37:24,400
I wrote about 650 pages in 20 weeks.

750
00:37:25,620 --> 00:37:29,380
And mostly I'd roll out of bed at about seven in the morning and just start writing.

751
00:37:29,480 --> 00:37:30,440
It was my best time to write.

752
00:37:30,780 --> 00:37:34,660
And then I would write all morning, maybe eat a little bit of cereal or something.

753
00:37:35,120 --> 00:37:36,180
Muesli, I'd eat a little muesli.

754
00:37:36,180 --> 00:37:39,380
And then I'd go and get lunch.

755
00:37:40,200 --> 00:37:44,380
Meet some of the assistants at the university, practice my Polish.

756
00:37:44,860 --> 00:37:52,540
And then in the afternoon, I would do proofreading of whatever I had just written or maybe the day before, whatever it was, I would do proofreading until dinner time.

757
00:37:52,960 --> 00:37:55,920
And then I would start reading materials.

758
00:37:56,040 --> 00:38:00,900
I brought a lot of materials with me and I would try to read and start to concentrate on what the next part was I was going to write the next day.

759
00:38:01,140 --> 00:38:07,300
And then by about nine or ten at night, I would drink a big beer and then pass out.

760
00:38:08,000 --> 00:38:09,160
I'd be dreaming about it.

761
00:38:09,160 --> 00:38:11,240
I definitely work a lot when I sleep.

762
00:38:11,800 --> 00:38:14,240
So things would be kind of somehow crystallized when I got up in the morning.

763
00:38:14,340 --> 00:38:16,080
I'd roll out of bed and the process just kept repeating.

764
00:38:16,100 --> 00:38:17,460
So basically total immersion.

765
00:38:17,740 --> 00:38:18,600
It was like that, yeah.

766
00:38:18,740 --> 00:38:19,720
And things were clearer.

767
00:38:20,300 --> 00:38:23,560
These concepts were clearer to me than anything I'd ever been in my life.

768
00:38:23,720 --> 00:38:24,660
It was so beautiful.

769
00:38:25,140 --> 00:38:29,920
But it was very antisocial and destructive in a lot of ways because I couldn't do that forever.

770
00:38:30,300 --> 00:38:32,980
But during that time, the clarity was just amazing.

771
00:38:33,120 --> 00:38:37,080
And there was never like a time when you thought, okay, I don't want to do this anymore.

772
00:38:37,200 --> 00:38:38,120
I want to give up here.

773
00:38:38,120 --> 00:38:39,920
No, there's a lot of stress.

774
00:38:39,980 --> 00:38:41,200
Am I going to be on schedule or not?

775
00:38:41,300 --> 00:38:42,940
Some of those chapters I wrote really fast.

776
00:38:43,000 --> 00:38:45,380
It was about two weeks per chapter I was trying to go there.

777
00:38:45,440 --> 00:38:49,400
But some of them got harder and some of them were easier.

778
00:38:49,500 --> 00:38:51,100
I was able to catch up and it was kind of difficult.

779
00:38:51,280 --> 00:38:57,400
And then I went back to Illinois and then wrote the remaining third or so later in that year.

780
00:38:57,600 --> 00:38:59,060
Except for the information space chapter.

781
00:38:59,060 --> 00:39:00,500
That was the hardest to write by far.

782
00:39:01,220 --> 00:39:04,580
And I finished that in 2015 sometime when we were getting kind of near the end.

783
00:39:04,840 --> 00:39:05,580
And the introduction.

784
00:39:05,740 --> 00:39:06,320
But that wasn't hard.

785
00:39:06,320 --> 00:39:07,640
I just saved it for last.

786
00:39:08,120 --> 00:39:10,640
And how did you actually get this book published?

787
00:39:10,820 --> 00:39:11,880
I mean, how did you get it?

788
00:39:11,960 --> 00:39:13,000
How did you find the publisher?

789
00:39:13,180 --> 00:39:16,560
How did you convince them that it was actually a good idea to write such a book?

790
00:39:16,720 --> 00:39:17,240
Yeah, very good question.

791
00:39:17,500 --> 00:39:20,280
I was always posting it on the internet for free when it was just course notes.

792
00:39:20,820 --> 00:39:25,500
And I thought at that time, when you search for things, you know, Google search was not localized.

793
00:39:25,840 --> 00:39:31,460
And the EDU domain, which my university was on, came up very high in searches.

794
00:39:31,760 --> 00:39:34,280
So I felt I'm going to put stuff on the internet and get my word out.

795
00:39:35,180 --> 00:39:37,180
I got used to using the internet that way in Iowa.

796
00:39:37,320 --> 00:39:38,100
I had moved to Illinois.

797
00:39:38,140 --> 00:39:39,100
By 2001.

798
00:39:39,320 --> 00:39:41,560
But I still had this mentality of just putting it on the internet.

799
00:39:41,640 --> 00:39:42,340
So it was already there.

800
00:39:42,740 --> 00:39:44,000
95% of the way done.

801
00:39:44,480 --> 00:39:47,600
And I just contacted the editor-in-chief for the right division.

802
00:39:47,740 --> 00:39:50,880
I think it was Computer Science and Mathematics for Cambridge University Press.

803
00:39:50,920 --> 00:39:51,700
That was my first choice.

804
00:39:52,220 --> 00:39:58,500
One reason why was because there was a book by Alan Hatcher, the mathematician, who I think he's at Cornell.

805
00:39:59,080 --> 00:40:00,500
I might be wrong.

806
00:40:00,980 --> 00:40:04,280
And he published his algebraic topology book for free.

807
00:40:04,520 --> 00:40:06,120
And it was, I mean, it was online for free.

808
00:40:06,160 --> 00:40:07,480
And he published it with Cambridge.

809
00:40:07,480 --> 00:40:07,840
And so I said, well, I'm going to put it on the internet.

810
00:40:07,840 --> 00:40:07,860
And he published it with Cambridge.

811
00:40:07,860 --> 00:40:08,080
And so I said, well, I'm going to put it on the internet.

812
00:40:08,100 --> 00:40:10,140
And he said, hey, can I get the Alan Hatcher deal, whatever that was?

813
00:40:10,520 --> 00:40:13,780
And they checked it out and replied back in a day or two and said, yeah.

814
00:40:14,380 --> 00:40:15,000
So that was easy.

815
00:40:15,620 --> 00:40:17,600
If not, I was going to go through a lot of other plans.

816
00:40:17,660 --> 00:40:20,740
I was going to look for a local publisher, like even a little regional publisher,

817
00:40:20,840 --> 00:40:25,580
do anything I could to get that book out there without just having all this money going to the publishers and such.

818
00:40:25,820 --> 00:40:27,780
I wanted it to be accessible to everyone.

819
00:40:27,840 --> 00:40:32,200
But I thought a printed copy, especially in that time period, would be really important to people.

820
00:40:32,200 --> 00:40:38,080
And so I think there were almost like a thousand references which he had in the book.

821
00:40:38,100 --> 00:40:41,560
So how did you actually manage to go through all those papers?

822
00:40:41,740 --> 00:40:45,560
How did you actually organize yourself to retain this information?

823
00:40:45,920 --> 00:40:48,780
Well, that was everything I accumulated going all the way back to my PhD.

824
00:40:48,840 --> 00:40:50,440
And I was very scholarly for PhD.

825
00:40:50,560 --> 00:40:53,900
I spent a lot of time in the library, a lot of time always trying to build a landscape.

826
00:40:54,100 --> 00:40:55,660
I call it like a landscape of literature.

827
00:40:56,420 --> 00:41:02,080
And very often, to go into a new area that I don't know as much about, the first paper is very painful, very hard to read.

828
00:41:02,360 --> 00:41:04,840
But I was falling back on all that mathematics that I mentioned before.

829
00:41:04,920 --> 00:41:07,280
So things are kind of easy, unless it's a poorly written paper.

830
00:41:07,280 --> 00:41:07,620
A lot of...

831
00:41:07,620 --> 00:41:09,460
The engineering papers are kind of showy.

832
00:41:10,040 --> 00:41:11,940
But I'm like, oh, that's just cumbersome notation.

833
00:41:12,100 --> 00:41:14,580
They kind of show off or they don't quite know how to express things right.

834
00:41:14,900 --> 00:41:15,700
I skip that stuff.

835
00:41:15,780 --> 00:41:18,180
I try to find the really good papers, learn the stuff well.

836
00:41:18,260 --> 00:41:24,640
And then if there's other papers that I want to reference that kind of fall into that, I can skim them a little more quickly and kind of say, oh, I can see what they're doing.

837
00:41:24,680 --> 00:41:26,000
And I see how it's related to these things.

838
00:41:26,280 --> 00:41:28,100
So I can give the right recommendation to the author.

839
00:41:28,400 --> 00:41:36,220
If you want this variant or that variant or these people showed this bound, you know, so these kind of things, just enough to make sure my statement is correct and where it fits and everything was like that.

840
00:41:36,220 --> 00:41:37,100
So I didn't just...

841
00:41:37,100 --> 00:41:39,160
At the start of writing the book, sit down and read a thousand papers.

842
00:41:39,320 --> 00:41:43,260
But it was something accumulated over probably a decade, more than a decade leading up to that.

843
00:41:44,120 --> 00:41:45,280
And I...

844
00:41:45,280 --> 00:41:49,720
So I guess all the papers were not like in PDF format.

845
00:41:50,140 --> 00:41:50,860
No, they weren't.

846
00:41:50,960 --> 00:41:52,600
I had to like print all of them.

847
00:41:52,820 --> 00:41:52,980
You're right.

848
00:41:53,080 --> 00:41:54,220
Like make big...

849
00:41:54,880 --> 00:41:55,820
I had copies of it.

850
00:41:56,100 --> 00:41:58,740
I had a whole suitcase just filled with books and papers and stuff.

851
00:41:58,740 --> 00:41:59,760
So that was very frustrating.

852
00:42:00,180 --> 00:42:01,800
And I kept going back to the US every...

853
00:42:01,800 --> 00:42:06,860
I went six weeks in Poland, one week back, six weeks in Poland, one week back, and then eight weeks in Poland.

854
00:42:07,000 --> 00:42:07,080
So I...

855
00:42:07,100 --> 00:42:08,680
I could exchange stuff as well.

856
00:42:10,000 --> 00:42:10,360
Nice.

857
00:42:10,500 --> 00:42:20,020
And do you think if you look back at the book, are there some parts which you find a little bit like deprecated right now or you would like rewrite in hindsight?

858
00:42:20,480 --> 00:42:20,660
Yeah.

859
00:42:20,740 --> 00:42:22,440
Like, do I want to cringe when I read something back?

860
00:42:22,820 --> 00:42:26,880
That was my biggest fear when I was writing it is that I would look back 10 or 20 years later.

861
00:42:26,960 --> 00:42:27,520
I kid you not.

862
00:42:27,920 --> 00:42:28,440
And have this...

863
00:42:28,440 --> 00:42:32,140
So there was like my future self imagined looking back at me going, don't screw this up.

864
00:42:32,180 --> 00:42:32,820
Don't screw this up.

865
00:42:33,000 --> 00:42:33,680
It'll be embarrassing.

866
00:42:33,680 --> 00:42:37,060
And I think because it was mostly fundamental stuff and mostly mathematical.

867
00:42:37,100 --> 00:42:40,160
And things like this that I could...

868
00:42:40,160 --> 00:42:42,920
Well, that's what I decided to do and not be too speculative about anything.

869
00:42:43,300 --> 00:42:43,720
There's no...

870
00:42:43,720 --> 00:42:45,140
I don't think there's much speculation in there.

871
00:42:45,760 --> 00:42:48,980
And so I don't have any regrets about what's written there.

872
00:42:49,080 --> 00:42:49,860
There's stuff I would add.

873
00:42:51,080 --> 00:43:02,280
Happy and proud of the fact that I covered quite a bit of reinforcement learning and worked that right into my coverage of value iteration and optimal control kind of form of planning and stuff.

874
00:43:02,320 --> 00:43:02,920
It fits perfectly.

875
00:43:03,160 --> 00:43:06,920
I guess I could add now instead of like grid-based representations for cost...

876
00:43:07,100 --> 00:43:09,040
Cost-to-go functions, we could have neural representations.

877
00:43:09,040 --> 00:43:11,080
And I could easily tie into that.

878
00:43:11,080 --> 00:43:16,180
I could add asymptotically optimal planners like RRT-STAR and a bunch of other things that came after that.

879
00:43:17,380 --> 00:43:20,540
And afterwards, you also published two more books, I think.

880
00:43:20,700 --> 00:43:20,860
Yeah.

881
00:43:21,360 --> 00:43:24,600
So did it actually become easier to write books?

882
00:43:25,180 --> 00:43:29,800
And what were maybe some lessons which you learned during this period of writing books?

883
00:43:29,900 --> 00:43:33,860
Well, it became harder because I have more time spent on family.

884
00:43:34,000 --> 00:43:36,080
Some challenging family issues, but also children.

885
00:43:36,300 --> 00:43:36,860
Children.

886
00:43:37,100 --> 00:43:37,960
And other things.

887
00:43:38,240 --> 00:43:41,800
So it got harder to really block off the time to do that.

888
00:43:42,940 --> 00:43:45,500
The sensing and filtering book is kind of small.

889
00:43:45,620 --> 00:43:47,940
It was like a teaser book for the bigger book that was supposed to come.

890
00:43:48,420 --> 00:43:52,960
And I was set to write that on sabbatical in Finland in 2012.

891
00:43:53,640 --> 00:43:57,160
Also, there was due to be an RRT book, which I wrote a part of.

892
00:43:58,000 --> 00:44:00,940
And my co-authors got busy in companies and stuff.

893
00:44:00,980 --> 00:44:02,480
And then I got busy in companies and stuff.

894
00:44:02,540 --> 00:44:03,340
And a lot of stuff happened.

895
00:44:03,340 --> 00:44:05,920
So that one might one day be revived.

896
00:44:05,980 --> 00:44:06,340
Who knows?

897
00:44:06,580 --> 00:44:06,900
But...

898
00:44:07,100 --> 00:44:09,820
But then after the VR thing, I wrote a VR book.

899
00:44:10,000 --> 00:44:13,320
That was a lot easier to write than the...

900
00:44:13,320 --> 00:44:16,380
It has a lot more words and pictures, put it that way, than the planning algorithm of the book.

901
00:44:16,500 --> 00:44:17,180
So I could kind of...

902
00:44:17,180 --> 00:44:18,880
And it's shorter, so I could pull that off more quickly.

903
00:44:18,880 --> 00:44:23,720
So you still have an RRT book on your shelf, which you want to publish at some point?

904
00:44:23,920 --> 00:44:26,440
Yeah, in a Linux directory somewhere.

905
00:44:27,920 --> 00:44:28,440
Nice.

906
00:44:29,140 --> 00:44:35,580
Yeah, and I guess almost everyone who likes to write also normally likes to read.

907
00:44:35,920 --> 00:44:36,320
So...

908
00:44:36,320 --> 00:44:36,940
Yeah.

909
00:44:36,940 --> 00:44:41,820
What are maybe like some books which you really like to read or maybe like some kind of books?

910
00:44:42,560 --> 00:44:43,800
Yeah, that's a good question.

911
00:44:43,860 --> 00:44:46,780
I'm not a super reader in some sense.

912
00:44:46,780 --> 00:44:48,720
I actually write better than I read.

913
00:44:48,860 --> 00:44:49,060
I don't know.

914
00:44:49,080 --> 00:44:50,540
My reading rate's always been kind of slow.

915
00:44:50,720 --> 00:44:52,060
And I don't know all the reasons for that.

916
00:44:52,080 --> 00:44:58,260
A lot of my family members have struggles with like ADD, ADHD, and different kinds of things like that.

917
00:44:58,360 --> 00:45:00,940
So my attention wanders quickly sometimes.

918
00:45:01,920 --> 00:45:02,620
I liked...

919
00:45:02,620 --> 00:45:05,560
In my 20s, I was kind of able to get my reading speed up enough to...

920
00:45:05,560 --> 00:45:06,740
When I took breaks from my research.

921
00:45:06,940 --> 00:45:08,040
I was able to read a lot of novels.

922
00:45:08,220 --> 00:45:10,100
I loved like Asimov's robot novels.

923
00:45:10,480 --> 00:45:12,940
I loved Arthur C. Clarke's books as well.

924
00:45:13,080 --> 00:45:13,620
Greg Baer.

925
00:45:13,760 --> 00:45:16,180
A lot of other like hard science fiction.

926
00:45:16,360 --> 00:45:17,140
So that was really nice.

927
00:45:17,620 --> 00:45:20,400
In terms of technical books, I love math books.

928
00:45:20,560 --> 00:45:21,660
And I collect math books.

929
00:45:22,080 --> 00:45:24,560
I have just rows and rows and rows of math books.

930
00:45:24,800 --> 00:45:26,360
And I was able to...

931
00:45:26,360 --> 00:45:30,660
Luckily, as a professor, I was able to buy them on my budget, like unrestricted money, and bring them with me.

932
00:45:30,680 --> 00:45:31,380
So I still have them.

933
00:45:31,620 --> 00:45:32,940
I have a home office with tons of them.

934
00:45:32,940 --> 00:45:35,760
Many of them are yellow books, like Springer Verlag.

935
00:45:36,940 --> 00:45:39,560
ETMs, graduate texts in mathematics, and undergrad ones as well.

936
00:45:39,840 --> 00:45:41,900
A lot of them are missing now because I hand them out to students.

937
00:45:42,040 --> 00:45:45,720
And then we both forget to have them come back to me and stuff like that.

938
00:45:46,220 --> 00:45:49,300
But I could name some of those kind of books maybe.

939
00:45:49,540 --> 00:45:50,860
But a lot of math books anyway.

940
00:45:51,340 --> 00:45:56,160
Is there maybe some book in particular which really influenced you and your life?

941
00:45:56,360 --> 00:45:58,340
And maybe the trajectory of your life even?

942
00:46:00,140 --> 00:46:01,100
Yeah, that's a good question.

943
00:46:01,100 --> 00:46:04,480
I can't say there's any one book like that.

944
00:46:04,980 --> 00:46:06,340
When I was an assistant professor,

945
00:46:06,340 --> 00:46:09,460
I read Ayn Rand's Fountainhead.

946
00:46:09,900 --> 00:46:13,460
But I don't get into sort of the politics and philosophy of Ayn Rand and that kind of thing.

947
00:46:13,760 --> 00:46:18,420
But I found the book very inspiring because there was one character who was super passionate and creative,

948
00:46:18,700 --> 00:46:24,580
and another character who was more obsessed with success and always needed the creative guy's help to do this.

949
00:46:24,980 --> 00:46:27,860
And Howard Rourke, I think, was the hero of that.

950
00:46:27,940 --> 00:46:29,340
And so Howard Rourke was like...

951
00:46:30,180 --> 00:46:32,500
It clearly separated things out for me.

952
00:46:32,580 --> 00:46:33,960
I wanted to be the one who's passionate.

953
00:46:33,960 --> 00:46:35,700
And I'll go down.

954
00:46:35,700 --> 00:46:40,340
I'll go down being passionate, trying to do the right things before I'll try to make the choices to succeed better.

955
00:46:41,120 --> 00:46:45,100
And that helped me to think about those things when I was starting off as an assistant professor.

956
00:46:45,480 --> 00:46:48,060
But again, I don't go further into all the philosophy and all this.

957
00:46:49,300 --> 00:46:50,660
I forgot the name of the...

958
00:46:50,660 --> 00:46:51,520
Anyway, I forgot.

959
00:46:51,700 --> 00:46:53,100
Yeah, anyway, not into the philosophy.

960
00:46:53,360 --> 00:46:58,160
In 2012, you actually got an email that changed a little bit the trajectory of your life.

961
00:46:58,720 --> 00:47:00,440
You joined Oculus.

962
00:47:01,080 --> 00:47:04,440
And so how did this actually happen that you switched to industry?

963
00:47:05,700 --> 00:47:06,760
What was the Oculus head sense?

964
00:47:07,160 --> 00:47:08,760
Yeah, that was quite an out-of-the-blue thing.

965
00:47:08,820 --> 00:47:10,740
And that disrupted my project to make more books.

966
00:47:10,940 --> 00:47:12,220
I sometimes still regret.

967
00:47:12,600 --> 00:47:14,780
But yet, it was a pretty good path, so it was all right.

968
00:47:15,680 --> 00:47:18,200
Well, I had settled into sabbatical in Finland.

969
00:47:19,280 --> 00:47:19,960
The fall was coming.

970
00:47:19,980 --> 00:47:20,700
We were very happy.

971
00:47:20,780 --> 00:47:23,120
I was there with my wife and two boys.

972
00:47:24,260 --> 00:47:25,740
And we really enjoyed it.

973
00:47:25,860 --> 00:47:27,380
But actually, my wife was a little bored.

974
00:47:27,520 --> 00:47:28,980
She has a PhD in robotics as well.

975
00:47:29,260 --> 00:47:32,120
And so she was looking for stuff to do.

976
00:47:32,880 --> 00:47:34,680
And anyway, out of the blue, I get this email.

977
00:47:35,700 --> 00:47:38,600
It's this company that just got a couple million dollars in a Kickstarter.

978
00:47:38,780 --> 00:47:43,660
The email is from a guy named Jack McCauley, who's a UC Berkeley alum from electrical engineering back in the 80s.

979
00:47:44,380 --> 00:47:48,980
And basically, they needed help with Euler angles and quaternions.

980
00:47:48,980 --> 00:47:50,240
They were Googling for stuff like that.

981
00:47:50,260 --> 00:47:52,560
And he found my book, my planning algorithm's book.

982
00:47:53,280 --> 00:47:55,480
And I just Googled for the company and said,

983
00:47:55,660 --> 00:47:57,300
Oh, these guys are getting a lot of press.

984
00:47:57,340 --> 00:47:58,800
And there's a lot of buzz around this.

985
00:47:59,140 --> 00:48:00,220
I don't know anything about companies.

986
00:48:00,300 --> 00:48:01,440
And I usually want to steer clear.

987
00:48:02,000 --> 00:48:05,020
I was also at a point in my life where I was frustrated.

988
00:48:05,020 --> 00:48:08,680
I had gone through a divorce, had a lot of different problems financially and stuff.

989
00:48:08,800 --> 00:48:12,500
And so I thought, I really want to take care of my family well, my kids.

990
00:48:13,180 --> 00:48:18,100
My boys who were, I forgot their ages, were about 10-ish or so.

991
00:48:18,160 --> 00:48:18,820
They're three years apart.

992
00:48:18,920 --> 00:48:21,180
But then maybe 8 and 11 or 7 and 10.

993
00:48:21,700 --> 00:48:23,500
And I may have more kids.

994
00:48:23,760 --> 00:48:27,640
And I wanted to put myself in a better financial position for security.

995
00:48:28,280 --> 00:48:30,540
And I thought this was, and this looked fun and adventurous.

996
00:48:30,720 --> 00:48:34,320
And I remember my video game excitement back in teen years.

997
00:48:34,320 --> 00:48:36,920
And this was kind of like working with grown-up teenagers, kind of.

998
00:48:37,020 --> 00:48:39,480
Palmer Lucky himself was 19 when he started the company.

999
00:48:40,060 --> 00:48:41,140
And so I thought, this is kind of fun.

1000
00:48:41,200 --> 00:48:42,880
Maybe this is like a good chance to explore, like,

1001
00:48:42,940 --> 00:48:47,660
what if I had gone like the entrepreneurial route instead of professorial route?

1002
00:48:48,220 --> 00:48:50,280
And so it seemed exciting to try that.

1003
00:48:50,560 --> 00:48:51,860
And my wife was looking for things to do.

1004
00:48:51,920 --> 00:48:54,360
So I convinced them that we would just be a two-person consulting team.

1005
00:48:54,360 --> 00:48:54,680
I'm ready.

1006
00:48:54,920 --> 00:48:55,600
We work together.

1007
00:48:55,840 --> 00:48:57,820
And we started writing code for them.

1008
00:48:57,840 --> 00:48:59,100
She's a better coder than me anyway.

1009
00:48:59,140 --> 00:49:02,020
And she's really good at coding mathematical things.

1010
00:49:02,040 --> 00:49:02,800
She's very meticulous.

1011
00:49:02,800 --> 00:49:04,040
And so...

1012
00:49:04,320 --> 00:49:05,560
So we coded different parts.

1013
00:49:05,700 --> 00:49:07,680
We wrote like core math parts.

1014
00:49:07,840 --> 00:49:11,220
And then we started writing the core tracking parts, head tracking parts.

1015
00:49:11,540 --> 00:49:14,080
And then got into sensor calibration and all kinds of stuff.

1016
00:49:14,100 --> 00:49:18,300
We even had a robot arm in Finland with the first Oculus sensor board on it.

1017
00:49:18,640 --> 00:49:21,740
And I encouraged, I begged them to buy me a robot.

1018
00:49:21,960 --> 00:49:24,900
They were suspicious because they go, oh, sure, buy the roboticist a robot.

1019
00:49:25,000 --> 00:49:25,980
You just want to play with your robot.

1020
00:49:26,160 --> 00:49:26,400
No.

1021
00:49:27,040 --> 00:49:30,600
It's a perfect thing to do, to do very systematic, carefully controlled motions.

1022
00:49:30,760 --> 00:49:31,580
So we can...

1023
00:49:31,580 --> 00:49:34,100
So I know what the acceleration is supposed to be and what the angular velocity...

1024
00:49:34,320 --> 00:49:39,040
And then you have to calibrate these sensors better than they are in smartphones to get

1025
00:49:39,040 --> 00:49:40,820
them to work for something like head tracking.

1026
00:49:41,000 --> 00:49:41,700
That's what we figured.

1027
00:49:42,200 --> 00:49:46,100
So that was basically your main contribution to Oculus?

1028
00:49:46,340 --> 00:49:51,940
That was the main contribution at the time was the core math software and head tracking.

1029
00:49:52,400 --> 00:49:54,640
And then when I...

1030
00:49:54,880 --> 00:49:58,940
We did about six months of consulting work and then to get some shares and things like

1031
00:49:58,940 --> 00:50:02,340
that, let's say they strongly encouraged.

1032
00:50:02,660 --> 00:50:03,620
I think I had to move to Southern California.

1033
00:50:03,620 --> 00:50:03,640
Yeah.

1034
00:50:03,640 --> 00:50:03,700
Yeah.

1035
00:50:03,700 --> 00:50:03,720
Yeah.

1036
00:50:03,720 --> 00:50:03,780
Yeah.

1037
00:50:03,780 --> 00:50:03,800
Yeah.

1038
00:50:03,800 --> 00:50:03,860
Yeah.

1039
00:50:03,860 --> 00:50:03,880
Yeah.

1040
00:50:03,880 --> 00:50:03,940
Yeah.

1041
00:50:03,940 --> 00:50:04,000
Yeah.

1042
00:50:04,000 --> 00:50:04,020
Yeah.

1043
00:50:04,020 --> 00:50:04,040
Yeah.

1044
00:50:04,040 --> 00:50:04,140
Yeah.

1045
00:50:04,140 --> 00:50:04,180
Yeah.

1046
00:50:04,180 --> 00:50:04,240
Yeah.

1047
00:50:04,320 --> 00:50:04,360
Yeah.

1048
00:50:04,360 --> 00:50:04,380
Yeah.

1049
00:50:04,840 --> 00:50:05,920
Bail on the sabbatical.

1050
00:50:06,040 --> 00:50:06,860
It was a big mess.

1051
00:50:07,380 --> 00:50:08,060
Kids were crying.

1052
00:50:08,180 --> 00:50:09,100
They loved Finland so much.

1053
00:50:09,160 --> 00:50:10,140
They wanted to stay in school there.

1054
00:50:10,520 --> 00:50:10,920
But...

1055
00:50:10,920 --> 00:50:13,420
So I ended up moving straight to...

1056
00:50:13,420 --> 00:50:14,580
I think it was March of 2013.

1057
00:50:14,760 --> 00:50:17,480
We moved straight to Irvine, California.

1058
00:50:18,080 --> 00:50:23,040
And then there, I started working on more improved head tracking for the three-degree

1059
00:50:23,040 --> 00:50:24,660
of freedom system that was part of DK1.

1060
00:50:24,760 --> 00:50:30,400
And then for DK2, I worked on the six-degree of freedom tracking, but also got really heavily

1061
00:50:30,400 --> 00:50:31,300
into human perception.

1062
00:50:31,420 --> 00:50:34,300
I hired a couple of perceptual psychologists and we started working on all...

1063
00:50:34,300 --> 00:50:39,200
sort of aspects. And I played a kind of chief scientist role. And I was also helpful in

1064
00:50:39,200 --> 00:50:44,060
a number of things. I guess when they brought investors around too, there was, I think some

1065
00:50:44,060 --> 00:50:49,040
of the investors liked having a, you know, academic gray beard or whatever you want to call it, kind

1066
00:50:49,040 --> 00:50:54,080
of with a little more background and things like that. And do you believe that nowadays virtual

1067
00:50:54,080 --> 00:50:59,160
reality is almost solved or completely solved? Or do you think there's like some open research

1068
00:50:59,160 --> 00:51:03,900
questions which we need to solve? No, it's a very active and growing research field. The communities

1069
00:51:03,900 --> 00:51:09,240
of research, university research are growing. It's a mess. So there's quite a bit to do.

1070
00:51:09,560 --> 00:51:14,080
The headsets have not improved a lot since, I mean, they've improved some, but not by huge

1071
00:51:14,080 --> 00:51:19,200
leaps and bounds, I would say, given all the hundreds of billions of dollars and amounts

1072
00:51:19,200 --> 00:51:23,760
have been invested. One of the reasons why is because of nanophotonics, optical engineering

1073
00:51:23,760 --> 00:51:28,320
kinds of problems, wave guides and these kinds of things. It's hardcore applied physics. And it's

1074
00:51:28,320 --> 00:51:32,240
not chips. It's something else. And there's people who do it in the world. Finland, in fact, is very

1075
00:51:32,240 --> 00:51:33,840
good for these things because of...

1076
00:51:33,900 --> 00:51:38,860
Nokia culture and things. But these are very difficult things. And in robotics, we know that

1077
00:51:38,860 --> 00:51:44,920
robots tend to do really well when there's better components, right? So this is one thing that I

1078
00:51:44,920 --> 00:51:49,180
believe Dieter Fox mentioned in his keynote here, that once the sick laser became available,

1079
00:51:50,080 --> 00:51:56,780
the robot systems they could build just grew tremendously. For VR, we were able to make good

1080
00:51:56,780 --> 00:52:03,080
headsets because of the smartphone screens and the MEMS IMUs and the GPUs and mobile GPUs,

1081
00:52:03,100 --> 00:52:03,880
all these things. And so we're able to do a lot of things. And so I think it's a very difficult

1082
00:52:03,900 --> 00:52:07,300
thing to do. But there's not another generation of these kinds of things. So what are we going to do?

1083
00:52:07,360 --> 00:52:11,140
We can't just program our way through these. So that's going to involve a lot of advances and

1084
00:52:11,140 --> 00:52:16,560
things. But I think on the fundamental side, the advances are there. It's more of industry's turn

1085
00:52:16,560 --> 00:52:20,000
to find the right applications for these things and make the right products and these kinds of

1086
00:52:20,000 --> 00:52:23,160
things, which is... They're good at spending money, but they might not be always good at

1087
00:52:23,160 --> 00:52:26,040
finding the right direction. Talking about applications,

1088
00:52:26,380 --> 00:52:30,180
so what do you think would be one killer application for virtual reality?

1089
00:52:30,740 --> 00:52:33,880
I don't know. I think if I knew what it was, I would invest in it or start a company.

1090
00:52:33,900 --> 00:52:38,120
And do that. I don't want to start a company. Maybe not. I'll trick somebody else into doing it.

1091
00:52:38,700 --> 00:52:44,820
But one thing I'm excited about is telepresence, using virtual reality and robots. So tele-embodiment.

1092
00:52:45,120 --> 00:52:48,640
I think that's a kind of killer app, but it's not going to be the first one.

1093
00:52:49,080 --> 00:52:53,140
It'll be something more along the lines of a VR version of TikTok or something. Who knows?

1094
00:52:54,240 --> 00:52:58,540
And so you were still at Oculus when Facebook bought Oculus, right?

1095
00:52:58,680 --> 00:52:59,180
Yeah, that's correct.

1096
00:52:59,220 --> 00:53:00,460
So how was this time?

1097
00:53:00,460 --> 00:53:03,540
I went for one day of Facebook onboarding.

1098
00:53:03,900 --> 00:53:07,940
Then I went back to the University of Illinois. I went and traveled and gave a few talks on behalf

1099
00:53:07,940 --> 00:53:09,120
of the company around...

1100
00:53:09,120 --> 00:53:11,080
Wasn't it so horrible, this one day of onboarding?

1101
00:53:11,500 --> 00:53:15,860
No, it was a fun day, I guess. But I never really had any intention to stay there.

1102
00:53:16,260 --> 00:53:20,360
I had a big, I had a sweet golden handcuffs deal that I just walked away from.

1103
00:53:20,860 --> 00:53:25,880
But I own some shares in Oculus anyway. So, you know, I...

1104
00:53:25,880 --> 00:53:26,520
You didn't really care.

1105
00:53:26,960 --> 00:53:30,140
Yeah, I mean, it was a lot that I walked away from, but I already got a lot. So

1106
00:53:30,220 --> 00:53:33,620
I wanted to... What's very important to me is to have control over...

1107
00:53:33,620 --> 00:53:37,460
Control over my time, you know, what I spend my time on. I'm mostly curiosity driven.

1108
00:53:38,080 --> 00:53:42,580
And so for my work, it's very important. I don't... I lose my passion right away if I'm just kind of told what to do all day,

1109
00:53:42,860 --> 00:53:45,440
or if other people are making decisions that are out of my control and things.

1110
00:53:45,780 --> 00:53:52,140
And I want to spend time with my family and enjoy life. And, you know, I could have worked there for five years to go through all the kind of process like a lot of other people did.

1111
00:53:52,140 --> 00:53:54,520
But I was certainly offered everything, but yeah.

1112
00:53:55,380 --> 00:53:58,820
So, and then basically went back to academic life.

1113
00:53:59,060 --> 00:54:01,880
Yeah, well, not right away, because things got crazy.

1114
00:54:01,880 --> 00:54:03,520
Because an Oculus was purchased for $3,000.

1115
00:54:03,620 --> 00:54:04,220
$3 billion.

1116
00:54:04,620 --> 00:54:10,120
Then a lot of things happened. I started doing angel investing. A lot of companies wanted advice.

1117
00:54:10,860 --> 00:54:12,120
I met with some billionaires.

1118
00:54:13,880 --> 00:54:21,200
And that changed a lot of things. People from Hollywood, like just a lot of different doors were open. So I explored as much as I could.

1119
00:54:21,380 --> 00:54:22,500
I was from Hollywood.

1120
00:54:22,640 --> 00:54:32,300
Yeah, I met Brett Leonard, for example, who was the director who created Lawnmower Man, the film about VR from the 90s.

1121
00:54:33,060 --> 00:54:33,560
We also met a lot of people. Yeah.

1122
00:54:33,620 --> 00:54:39,520
I met a lot of other people when we were in Oculus. But these are people that he was already reading the VR book that I had online.

1123
00:54:40,300 --> 00:54:49,080
And he had a small company going with a couple of other people from Hollywood. Not actors, but people who actually, you know, produce things and such.

1124
00:54:49,080 --> 00:54:55,920
And they were trying to do VR storytelling kinds of things. And they liked what they read in my book, my VR book.

1125
00:54:56,680 --> 00:55:01,960
And so we started doing some things like that. But then, you know, the whole ecosystem was kind of going down kind of quickly.

1126
00:55:02,120 --> 00:55:03,600
So it was around 2015, 2016.

1127
00:55:03,620 --> 00:55:15,460
I worked for Huawei for a while as a vice president and chief scientist of VR, AR, MR. That was fascinating. Spent a lot of time in China and US and Finland.

1128
00:55:15,620 --> 00:55:21,600
There's different branches of Huawei all over the place. I learned a lot about Chinese culture and technology there. That was wonderful.

1129
00:55:22,520 --> 00:55:26,420
But I ultimately left that kind of fast because I wanted to focus more on my family.

1130
00:55:27,620 --> 00:55:30,080
Yep. And then eventually just went back to university completely.

1131
00:55:30,480 --> 00:55:33,080
And I think they also wanted to invest into big...

1132
00:55:33,620 --> 00:55:36,380
I think our building is near the campus or something, right?

1133
00:55:37,560 --> 00:55:38,360
In which place?

1134
00:55:39,080 --> 00:55:40,300
In the US, I think.

1135
00:55:41,540 --> 00:55:42,980
Yeah, I don't know about that. University of Illinois?

1136
00:55:43,800 --> 00:55:52,660
Yeah, I think I read this somewhere that Huawei wanted to invest there, but then somehow this deal actually fell through.

1137
00:55:52,660 --> 00:55:54,160
Yeah, yeah, that's right. That's right.

1138
00:55:54,220 --> 00:56:00,720
Well, it was right on the time where the university was encouraging me to, and everyone, to do lots of cooperation with China.

1139
00:56:00,880 --> 00:56:02,660
You know, they're putting campuses there, doing all kinds of things.

1140
00:56:03,080 --> 00:56:03,600
And I said,

1141
00:56:03,640 --> 00:56:07,620
Well, Huawei invited me to be some high-ranking person.

1142
00:56:07,780 --> 00:56:12,680
In fact, I was in meetings where it's just all Chinese people and all speaking Chinese all day.

1143
00:56:12,800 --> 00:56:14,300
And I'm the only... I don't know Chinese language.

1144
00:56:14,560 --> 00:56:18,140
And they're very nice, but they weren't very international at that level.

1145
00:56:18,260 --> 00:56:19,280
They're a very international company.

1146
00:56:19,420 --> 00:56:21,660
And I have lots of great things to say about them.

1147
00:56:21,880 --> 00:56:25,820
But at that level, I was the highest-ranking non-Chinese person around and stuff.

1148
00:56:26,000 --> 00:56:28,200
So it was a very good opportunity.

1149
00:56:29,480 --> 00:56:31,600
But I lost my... Sorry, go back to your question again, because I think I...

1150
00:56:32,260 --> 00:56:33,600
Well, somehow...

1151
00:56:34,140 --> 00:56:35,900
They were like a political tension.

1152
00:56:35,980 --> 00:56:36,540
Oh, yes, about that. Yeah.

1153
00:56:36,620 --> 00:56:39,520
So then it all kind of... Yeah, then it got kind of bad.

1154
00:56:39,700 --> 00:56:42,060
So the university very quickly changed its stance.

1155
00:56:42,940 --> 00:56:46,740
And I think they were under some pressure to do that from the government and things like this.

1156
00:56:46,840 --> 00:56:48,260
And so, so fine, you know.

1157
00:56:49,180 --> 00:56:53,660
Shortly after that time, I left and moved to Finland anyway for a huge number of reasons.

1158
00:56:53,960 --> 00:56:56,620
But that was a factor.

1159
00:56:56,660 --> 00:56:58,300
I left Huawei at the time, too, as well.

1160
00:56:58,300 --> 00:57:00,600
For a while, I tried to get Huawei to transfer me to Finland.

1161
00:57:01,520 --> 00:57:02,860
But that was very confusing for them.

1162
00:57:02,940 --> 00:57:03,600
They either transferred me...

1163
00:57:03,640 --> 00:57:07,040
People from China to country X or country X to China.

1164
00:57:07,140 --> 00:57:09,440
But they never transferred somebody from country X to country Y.

1165
00:57:09,880 --> 00:57:11,520
They said that makes no sense.

1166
00:57:11,600 --> 00:57:14,540
I'm like, OK, well, I can't keep working for you from America.

1167
00:57:14,680 --> 00:57:14,860
Sorry.

1168
00:57:15,140 --> 00:57:15,300
Yeah.

1169
00:57:16,040 --> 00:57:16,300
OK.

1170
00:57:16,440 --> 00:57:19,940
And so then you basically went back to academic life.

1171
00:57:20,100 --> 00:57:20,940
Went back to academic life.

1172
00:57:21,020 --> 00:57:23,620
Full swing, full force in Finland then.

1173
00:57:23,680 --> 00:57:23,920
Yeah.

1174
00:57:24,300 --> 00:57:24,500
Cool.

1175
00:57:24,500 --> 00:57:28,360
So I would like to talk a little bit about doing successful research.

1176
00:57:28,520 --> 00:57:31,760
So I think you are like one of the most exceptional.

1177
00:57:32,520 --> 00:57:33,500
You had a very exceptional...

1178
00:57:33,600 --> 00:57:35,000
You had a very exceptional research career, I would say.

1179
00:57:35,320 --> 00:57:37,380
What do you think were actually the biggest...

1180
00:57:37,380 --> 00:57:39,120
Had the biggest impact on the success?

1181
00:57:39,840 --> 00:57:43,300
And is there maybe someone who really deeply influenced you?

1182
00:57:45,160 --> 00:57:46,620
Yeah, it's really a lot of people.

1183
00:57:46,780 --> 00:57:50,720
I mean, I could cite just a couple of critical teachers in high school that inspired me.

1184
00:57:50,820 --> 00:57:52,060
I had a great PhD advisor.

1185
00:57:53,340 --> 00:57:57,980
And just lots of conversations with researchers and conferences.

1186
00:57:58,220 --> 00:58:00,180
Or they visited us at Illinois or Stanford.

1187
00:58:00,180 --> 00:58:03,040
I don't think there's any one kind of thing.

1188
00:58:03,040 --> 00:58:04,420
It's just a lot of...

1189
00:58:04,420 --> 00:58:07,560
I think I was a very shy person and worked very hard.

1190
00:58:07,620 --> 00:58:08,620
But it was very hard for me socially.

1191
00:58:08,880 --> 00:58:14,960
And so I think trying to overcome those fears and trying to get myself out there a little more.

1192
00:58:15,240 --> 00:58:16,100
It was slow going.

1193
00:58:16,540 --> 00:58:22,340
But everyone has to work on their strengths and try to fix their weaknesses and not let those stop them.

1194
00:58:22,620 --> 00:58:23,680
And so those were challenges.

1195
00:58:23,800 --> 00:58:24,700
But I was able to get through those.

1196
00:58:24,760 --> 00:58:27,940
But ultimately, I got to know enough people and found many inspiring researchers.

1197
00:58:27,940 --> 00:58:31,940
And so what kind of attributes do you think successful research are really...

1198
00:58:33,040 --> 00:58:33,540
Needs to have?

1199
00:58:34,620 --> 00:58:35,860
Well, you have to love what you do.

1200
00:58:36,020 --> 00:58:36,940
Have a great passion.

1201
00:58:37,140 --> 00:58:42,440
I think the passion is more important than wanting to win the prizes or wanting to get the great...

1202
00:58:42,440 --> 00:58:45,720
You know, we like to have a secure job where you can keep doing research.

1203
00:58:45,960 --> 00:58:48,560
But I think one has to be careful about gaming it too much.

1204
00:58:48,760 --> 00:58:53,300
So I think for me, given the background that I had come from growing up,

1205
00:58:53,540 --> 00:58:58,040
I was already at such high levels that I'm not afraid to lose my tenure track position or whatever.

1206
00:58:58,040 --> 00:58:59,040
I just can't...

1207
00:59:00,000 --> 00:59:01,340
I'm going to do what I think is the right thing.

1208
00:59:01,760 --> 00:59:02,920
And I'm much more...

1209
00:59:03,040 --> 00:59:05,200
A tolerant of risk than a lot of people, perhaps,

1210
00:59:05,260 --> 00:59:07,800
because I think the levels I went to were much higher than I imagined anyway.

1211
00:59:07,880 --> 00:59:09,420
I'm just like, let's just see how far this goes.

1212
00:59:09,560 --> 00:59:11,360
You know, it's not double or nothing, double or nothing.

1213
00:59:11,520 --> 00:59:12,680
It's double, double, double.

1214
00:59:12,760 --> 00:59:15,400
And then I'll fall back on something that's still pretty darn good.

1215
00:59:15,740 --> 00:59:20,420
So I think it's important to be able to take risks and stick close to your passion.

1216
00:59:20,700 --> 00:59:22,860
And hopefully, you know, you have good values.

1217
00:59:22,980 --> 00:59:25,420
I love teaching and working with students.

1218
00:59:25,420 --> 00:59:26,260
That's also very important.

1219
00:59:26,260 --> 00:59:29,960
There's kind of like intellectual renewal going on all the time.

1220
00:59:30,880 --> 00:59:32,340
And I think also in the last...

1221
00:59:32,340 --> 00:59:32,500
I would say...

1222
00:59:33,040 --> 00:59:41,460
Maybe like 15 or 20 years, the age index became like really the biggest indicator of performance for research.

1223
00:59:41,740 --> 00:59:47,380
And what are your thoughts on the age index as such an important metric for research?

1224
00:59:47,460 --> 00:59:49,700
Is it maybe a bit too much?

1225
00:59:49,780 --> 00:59:53,700
Is it like something which is a good proxy for research?

1226
00:59:54,180 --> 00:59:56,140
I think it's random garbage.

1227
00:59:57,380 --> 00:59:58,520
It's random garbage.

1228
00:59:58,700 --> 01:00:00,540
It just looks like...

1229
01:00:00,540 --> 01:00:01,140
It looks like...

1230
01:00:01,840 --> 01:00:02,940
The older you get, the bigger...

1231
01:00:03,040 --> 01:00:03,580
The bigger it gets.

1232
01:00:03,720 --> 01:00:05,560
And the kind of busier you are, the bigger it gets.

1233
01:00:05,620 --> 01:00:12,800
So if you publish a lot, you got to be active enough to kind of, you know, you got to be in some community and get out there enough to like market your stuff, I guess.

1234
01:00:12,840 --> 01:00:14,120
And then you can get a kind of a big number.

1235
01:00:14,220 --> 01:00:15,160
But I don't know.

1236
01:00:16,060 --> 01:00:26,020
One interesting thing, P.R. Kumar, professor who was at Illinois for many years, and he worked in a stochastic control theory, wrote a book with Varaya on stochastic control.

1237
01:00:26,080 --> 01:00:26,560
Really nice book.

1238
01:00:27,380 --> 01:00:31,960
He was giving a lecture once to young graduate students, and he was explaining the academic career.

1239
01:00:32,040 --> 01:00:33,000
And he said something really cool.

1240
01:00:33,040 --> 01:00:34,300
It gets nerdy and cool.

1241
01:00:34,380 --> 01:00:41,720
He said that in the beginning of your career, people apply the L1 metric to it, which is basically just looking at the sum of the things you've done.

1242
01:00:42,360 --> 01:00:46,620
And then as you get older, they apply the L infinity metric, which is what's the maximum thing that you've done.

1243
01:00:47,140 --> 01:00:48,640
And I think that's a more interesting way.

1244
01:00:48,760 --> 01:00:54,480
It's harder to assess if you don't have the expertise, but that's a pretty good way to do it.

1245
01:00:54,560 --> 01:00:58,120
So you can be more kind of, did you publish in a bunch of prestigious places when you're junior?

1246
01:00:58,480 --> 01:00:59,200
That shows something.

1247
01:00:59,280 --> 01:01:01,120
It's hard to do that, and it shows the kind of productivity.

1248
01:01:01,300 --> 01:01:02,880
But only time tells, really.

1249
01:01:03,580 --> 01:01:07,780
Over 10 or 20 years, did you really have some big groundbreaking thing or something really big?

1250
01:01:07,780 --> 01:01:16,100
So if I'm done with research tomorrow, and I look back, and I have the RRT or something, that's a pretty big thing.

1251
01:01:16,160 --> 01:01:16,560
And that's cool.

1252
01:01:16,660 --> 01:01:20,140
And I have this kind of monster book that I'm quite proud of.

1253
01:01:20,200 --> 01:01:24,240
And if those are my L infinity metric, the things that I remember for, that's fine.

1254
01:01:24,500 --> 01:01:30,140
If my H index were 2, because I had, let's say, a landmark RRT paper in that book, that'd be fine.

1255
01:01:31,020 --> 01:01:31,420
No.

1256
01:01:31,420 --> 01:01:34,420
Well, that sounds good.

1257
01:01:35,800 --> 01:01:38,580
So let me talk a little bit about your time allocation.

1258
01:01:38,740 --> 01:01:43,340
I mean, you have so many things that you have to keep up at the same time.

1259
01:01:43,420 --> 01:01:44,000
You have to teach.

1260
01:01:44,060 --> 01:01:44,600
You have to write.

1261
01:01:44,660 --> 01:01:45,380
You have to read.

1262
01:01:45,580 --> 01:01:46,920
You have to supervise students.

1263
01:01:47,140 --> 01:01:48,140
You have to do research.

1264
01:01:48,400 --> 01:01:53,640
So what is actually a way of how you structure, for example, your day and how do you...

1265
01:01:53,640 --> 01:01:57,580
I'm a very unstructured person, and I don't do very well with schedules.

1266
01:01:57,580 --> 01:01:59,640
So I do try to...

1267
01:01:59,640 --> 01:02:01,000
So you don't have this daily habit of...

1268
01:02:01,000 --> 01:02:01,780
No.

1269
01:02:02,060 --> 01:02:03,700
...getting up at 5 a.m.?

1270
01:02:03,700 --> 01:02:04,180
No, no, no.

1271
01:02:04,640 --> 01:02:12,680
I always make sure I sleep enough, and I always try to make sure my students or people in my group know that I'm available and such.

1272
01:02:12,680 --> 01:02:16,320
But I like a model more where they push me when they want something.

1273
01:02:16,720 --> 01:02:17,380
I tell them that.

1274
01:02:17,440 --> 01:02:19,280
I'm not going to have regular meetings all the time.

1275
01:02:19,420 --> 01:02:20,480
And I don't chase people.

1276
01:02:20,700 --> 01:02:21,700
They need to come to me.

1277
01:02:22,080 --> 01:02:23,660
So people usually do well like that with me.

1278
01:02:24,780 --> 01:02:26,280
And things have changed over the years.

1279
01:02:26,480 --> 01:02:27,680
I think right now...

1280
01:02:28,480 --> 01:02:30,680
I'm not retired, but I'm in...

1281
01:02:30,680 --> 01:02:32,440
I like to say the tired phase of my career.

1282
01:02:32,560 --> 01:02:39,700
So before retired, I'm tired in the sense that I probably have more work-life balance than ever, and I'm spending a lot of time with my family.

1283
01:02:39,820 --> 01:02:41,160
I'm traveling typically less.

1284
01:02:42,200 --> 01:02:43,220
I wasn't sure whether I...

1285
01:02:43,220 --> 01:02:46,040
This is such an amazing event, but I had to really think about it.

1286
01:02:46,120 --> 01:02:47,500
I'm spending time away from my family.

1287
01:02:47,720 --> 01:02:50,420
And so I really try to balance things out in that direction.

1288
01:02:50,420 --> 01:02:59,680
And then I also have six, I think six right now, six or seven postdoctoral researchers, postdoc or higher researchers on my team.

1289
01:03:00,040 --> 01:03:00,520
And then...

1290
01:03:00,520 --> 01:03:00,560
And then...

1291
01:03:00,560 --> 01:03:00,660
And then...

1292
01:03:00,660 --> 01:03:04,580
And then they spend a lot of time working with students and we bounce ideas off of each other and things.

1293
01:03:04,640 --> 01:03:06,260
And so they handle a lot of things as well.

1294
01:03:06,720 --> 01:03:09,140
And also there's another colleague of mine named Timo Oyala.

1295
01:03:09,140 --> 01:03:12,640
He's a professor who runs the center that we're...

1296
01:03:12,640 --> 01:03:13,600
That our group is a part of.

1297
01:03:13,720 --> 01:03:15,080
And we kind of run the group together.

1298
01:03:15,420 --> 01:03:21,200
And he handles all of the expected like crazy bureaucratic things that I don't understand, half of which are in Finnish.

1299
01:03:21,460 --> 01:03:22,760
And that makes my life wonderful.

1300
01:03:22,760 --> 01:03:26,080
So I can just focus on research and teaching a bit.

1301
01:03:27,240 --> 01:03:30,540
And is there anything you believe like younger researchers...

1302
01:03:30,540 --> 01:03:32,540
And robotics should do differently or...

1303
01:03:32,540 --> 01:03:34,540
Well, I don't...

1304
01:03:34,540 --> 01:03:37,800
I think it's fairly general thing to ask and everyone's different.

1305
01:03:38,480 --> 01:03:42,860
But I think, you know, make sure you play to your strengths and fix your weaknesses just generally.

1306
01:03:43,160 --> 01:03:49,800
And I would say, you know, don't think everything needs to be just deep learning and go with the herd and all these things.

1307
01:03:50,120 --> 01:03:53,480
I encourage everyone to dig deeper, you know, do some good scholarly work.

1308
01:03:53,520 --> 01:03:55,860
Whatever is analogous to when I used to go to the library.

1309
01:03:56,280 --> 01:03:57,340
You know, work hard to find things.

1310
01:03:57,340 --> 01:04:00,340
Don't just search and something falls into your lap and you assume that's...

1311
01:04:00,860 --> 01:04:02,000
That's the complete search on things.

1312
01:04:02,400 --> 01:04:06,600
And study as much fundamental mathematics the most as you possibly can.

1313
01:04:07,180 --> 01:04:08,340
And, you know, get good at those things.

1314
01:04:08,380 --> 01:04:09,680
Because those are much harder to learn later.

1315
01:04:10,180 --> 01:04:13,100
So do it while your brain is sharp and you have free time, not a lot of commitments.

1316
01:04:14,320 --> 01:04:18,600
So really like keeping your knowledge in mathematics is like one of the most important things.

1317
01:04:18,600 --> 01:04:19,240
I think so, yeah.

1318
01:04:19,320 --> 01:04:20,140
Younger people could do.

1319
01:04:20,360 --> 01:04:20,840
That's right.

1320
01:04:20,980 --> 01:04:22,240
If you're terrible at it, then fine.

1321
01:04:22,360 --> 01:04:25,620
You know, do more experimental work or do something else or change fields.

1322
01:04:25,620 --> 01:04:26,100
I don't know.

1323
01:04:26,280 --> 01:04:27,760
But at least for the way...

1324
01:04:27,760 --> 01:04:29,660
I can't say one thing fits everyone.

1325
01:04:30,540 --> 01:04:35,360
But definitely think about things you should learn now while you're not as encumbered.

1326
01:04:35,360 --> 01:04:38,080
And your body's younger and you can put more hours into things and stuff like that.

1327
01:04:38,080 --> 01:04:39,520
Because it's much harder later.

1328
01:04:40,220 --> 01:04:43,820
So in 2018, you actually moved permanently to Finland.

1329
01:04:44,120 --> 01:04:46,560
So what fascinates you about Finland?

1330
01:04:46,900 --> 01:04:48,000
Why did you make this move?

1331
01:04:48,440 --> 01:04:52,720
Yeah, well, you know, in the life of a researcher and professor, we get to travel all over the world.

1332
01:04:52,720 --> 01:04:55,720
And you get to hang out with people from your own research area.

1333
01:04:55,720 --> 01:05:00,520
So you almost imagine like what would life be like if I were living in this country?

1334
01:05:00,520 --> 01:05:05,520
And so probably these people you work with who also have the same passion for robotics or motion planning and stuff.

1335
01:05:05,520 --> 01:05:07,520
Probably that's what it would be like.

1336
01:05:07,520 --> 01:05:12,520
So I just, you know, I went to Europe a lot and I started from the sabbatical.

1337
01:05:12,520 --> 01:05:13,520
I lived in Poland.

1338
01:05:13,520 --> 01:05:14,520
I found that fascinating.

1339
01:05:14,520 --> 01:05:20,520
I found, you know, Germany's nice, Netherlands, UK, a lot of different countries.

1340
01:05:20,520 --> 01:05:23,520
But I kept finding the Nordic countries probably the most appealing somehow.

1341
01:05:23,520 --> 01:05:29,520
I like the sort of very advanced in technology, very clean, very...

1342
01:05:29,520 --> 01:05:30,020
Yeah.

1343
01:05:30,520 --> 01:05:32,520
Oriented towards preserving nature.

1344
01:05:32,520 --> 01:05:35,520
And I thought Sweden was very nice and Norway.

1345
01:05:35,520 --> 01:05:39,520
But Finland out of the end was probably the one that captivated me the most.

1346
01:05:39,520 --> 01:05:42,520
And it's in that same like Eastern European strip.

1347
01:05:42,520 --> 01:05:45,520
And so I like Poland and my mom came from Eastern Europe.

1348
01:05:45,520 --> 01:05:47,520
And so that was important.

1349
01:05:47,520 --> 01:05:58,520
My wife's Ukrainian and her parents were living with us in Illinois, even in California during Oculus because of the war in Ukraine from Kharkiv.

1350
01:05:58,520 --> 01:05:59,520
And so we had to get them out of there because it looked like they were going to be there.

1351
01:05:59,520 --> 01:06:04,520
We had to get them out of there because it looked like that city was going to go next the same way as the Donbas.

1352
01:06:04,520 --> 01:06:05,520
But it didn't.

1353
01:06:05,520 --> 01:06:06,520
Now it is.

1354
01:06:06,520 --> 01:06:07,520
Now it's being bombed all the time.

1355
01:06:07,520 --> 01:06:11,520
But then her father got ALS in the US.

1356
01:06:11,520 --> 01:06:12,520
And there was no way we could...

1357
01:06:12,520 --> 01:06:13,520
It was really hard for us to care for him.

1358
01:06:13,520 --> 01:06:17,520
Even with Oculus earnings, it's ridiculously expensive.

1359
01:06:17,520 --> 01:06:18,520
And they had good insurance.

1360
01:06:18,520 --> 01:06:20,520
But of course, nobody covers stuff like that.

1361
01:06:20,520 --> 01:06:28,520
And I just was so in love with the system in Finland where it's, you know, free education and healthcare and people are just very thoughtful and nice.

1362
01:06:28,520 --> 01:06:30,520
Not arrogant about things, but just very, very nice.

1363
01:06:30,520 --> 01:06:34,520
So we brought everyone, including my in-laws, to Finland.

1364
01:06:34,520 --> 01:06:37,520
And they're still caring for my wife's father.

1365
01:06:37,520 --> 01:06:39,520
And it's been six years.

1366
01:06:39,520 --> 01:06:43,520
And he had a prognosis of only living about a year in America.

1367
01:06:43,520 --> 01:06:44,520
And it's just very easy.

1368
01:06:44,520 --> 01:06:48,520
And he's in a care center that's attached to our apartment building.

1369
01:06:48,520 --> 01:06:52,520
So where my mother-in-law doesn't even have to go outside in the winter to see him every night and everything.

1370
01:06:52,520 --> 01:06:56,520
So it's just that really helps us get a lot of work done because things are just taken care of.

1371
01:06:56,520 --> 01:06:57,520
And it's just a great example of how they take care of us.

1372
01:06:57,520 --> 01:07:00,520
And I thought to teach my children.

1373
01:07:00,520 --> 01:07:01,520
I have four kids.

1374
01:07:01,520 --> 01:07:03,520
I have two adult kids who are there with us.

1375
01:07:03,520 --> 01:07:05,520
Well, my oldest son is studying in Switzerland now.

1376
01:07:05,520 --> 01:07:08,520
But the rest, he just moved away a month or two ago.

1377
01:07:08,520 --> 01:07:10,520
But they're all essentially there in Finland.

1378
01:07:10,520 --> 01:07:16,520
And I want them to learn those values that we should all take care of each other.

1379
01:07:16,520 --> 01:07:21,520
And you should not be trying to just get a pile of money so that you can get healthcare and education and these kind of things.

1380
01:07:21,520 --> 01:07:25,520
So I thought, okay, I made some money in America, but I just don't believe in the values.

1381
01:07:25,520 --> 01:07:26,520
I believe for my children.

1382
01:07:26,520 --> 01:07:27,520
I want them to know that.

1383
01:07:27,520 --> 01:07:29,520
I want them to learn these Finnish values.

1384
01:07:29,520 --> 01:07:31,520
And then maybe there's other countries with similar values.

1385
01:07:31,520 --> 01:07:33,520
But I thought the Finns implement that very nicely.

1386
01:07:33,520 --> 01:07:36,520
And they're very practical and not so arrogant about it.

1387
01:07:36,520 --> 01:07:39,520
They've never had a big imperial empire or anything like that.

1388
01:07:39,520 --> 01:07:41,520
They're just proud to have their country.

1389
01:07:41,520 --> 01:07:44,520
And they're even apologetic for speaking Finnish in their own country.

1390
01:07:44,520 --> 01:07:46,520
It's absurd.

1391
01:07:46,520 --> 01:07:49,520
So have you already learned a little bit of Finnish?

1392
01:07:49,520 --> 01:07:52,520
I heard that it's one of the most difficult languages.

1393
01:07:52,520 --> 01:07:53,520
It is.

1394
01:07:53,520 --> 01:07:55,520
It's quite outrageously hard.

1395
01:07:55,520 --> 01:07:56,520
And there's a lot of reasons for that.

1396
01:07:56,520 --> 01:07:58,520
Probably don't want to go into all the reasons here.

1397
01:07:58,520 --> 01:08:00,520
But I speak Finnish at a basic level.

1398
01:08:00,520 --> 01:08:02,520
But I'm still working on it all the time.

1399
01:08:02,520 --> 01:08:04,520
My seven-year-old girl is the best Finnish speaker in the family now.

1400
01:08:04,520 --> 01:08:05,520
She's perfect.

1401
01:08:05,520 --> 01:08:12,520
She speaks Russian from Ukrainian, Russian, and then English and Finnish all perfectly.

1402
01:08:12,520 --> 01:08:17,520
And is there still something about the U.S. which you miss, which you don't have in Finland?

1403
01:08:17,520 --> 01:08:18,520
Yeah.

1404
01:08:18,520 --> 01:08:25,520
I mean, I think the amount of diversity, especially in cities and in universities, cultural diversity, ethnic diversity, people from all over the place.

1405
01:08:25,520 --> 01:08:32,520
I really miss that, especially because it tends to lead to excellent restaurants that are made for the people from that country.

1406
01:08:32,520 --> 01:08:35,520
Like the Chinese food at the University of Illinois on the Champaign-Urbana campus.

1407
01:08:35,520 --> 01:08:40,520
We had something like 7,000, a rough estimate, of Chinese students there.

1408
01:08:40,520 --> 01:08:46,520
They had every different type of trendy, cool restaurant that I had seen in China that I learned about when I was at Huawei.

1409
01:08:46,520 --> 01:08:47,520
And it was there.

1410
01:08:47,520 --> 01:08:49,520
And I could just have that stuff and enjoy it.

1411
01:08:49,520 --> 01:08:54,520
And it was like that for every other country and culture and stuff, especially in the big cities and stuff.

1412
01:08:54,520 --> 01:08:55,520
So I miss that.

1413
01:08:55,520 --> 01:08:56,520
Yeah.

1414
01:08:56,520 --> 01:09:03,520
I saw once that you gave a talk at a big conference remotely from your sauna in Finland, which was very cool.

1415
01:09:03,520 --> 01:09:08,520
So what actually fascinates you about saunas and what kind of benefits do you get from them?

1416
01:09:08,520 --> 01:09:11,520
Well, sauna is a Finnish word.

1417
01:09:11,520 --> 01:09:12,520
I even say sauna.

1418
01:09:12,520 --> 01:09:13,520
That's how you say it in Finnish.

1419
01:09:13,520 --> 01:09:15,520
And it is a Finnish word.

1420
01:09:15,520 --> 01:09:16,520
I said it incorrectly.

1421
01:09:16,520 --> 01:09:20,520
So sauna is English, and that's the pronunciation of it.

1422
01:09:20,520 --> 01:09:22,520
And that's their gift to the English language, that word.

1423
01:09:22,520 --> 01:09:23,520
And it's like a report.

1424
01:09:23,520 --> 01:09:24,520
Yeah.

1425
01:09:24,520 --> 01:09:26,520
And it's like a reverse swimming pool.

1426
01:09:26,520 --> 01:09:29,520
So in some hot parts of the world, everyone has a swimming pool to jump in to cool off.

1427
01:09:29,520 --> 01:09:33,520
So in a cold part of the world, you jump in the sauna to heat back up again.

1428
01:09:33,520 --> 01:09:35,520
But it's also a very special place to Finns.

1429
01:09:35,520 --> 01:09:38,520
It's a place to, you know, you go in.

1430
01:09:38,520 --> 01:09:42,520
In my video, I wasn't naked, but usually you're supposed to be in the sauna naked.

1431
01:09:42,520 --> 01:09:43,520
Otherwise, it'll burn you.

1432
01:09:43,520 --> 01:09:48,520
It's 80 degrees Celsius in there with a hot steam coming over from the coals and everything.

1433
01:09:48,520 --> 01:09:50,520
And it's a place to connect with people.

1434
01:09:50,520 --> 01:09:52,520
And like a lot of people have business meetings and other kind of things there.

1435
01:09:52,520 --> 01:09:54,520
It's like a humbling kind of thing.

1436
01:09:54,520 --> 01:09:57,520
Is that the Finnish thing that you have business meetings in the sauna?

1437
01:09:57,520 --> 01:09:58,520
Yeah.

1438
01:09:58,520 --> 01:09:59,520
Nice.

1439
01:09:59,520 --> 01:10:00,520
That's right.

1440
01:10:00,520 --> 01:10:01,520
And so they'll bring visitors.

1441
01:10:01,520 --> 01:10:04,520
And of course, the Finns will chuckle a little because it's quite hot.

1442
01:10:04,520 --> 01:10:05,520
Yeah.

1443
01:10:05,520 --> 01:10:07,520
So it's a very important thing to them.

1444
01:10:07,520 --> 01:10:09,520
And I think it's an interesting part of their culture.

1445
01:10:09,520 --> 01:10:13,520
And I've come to, you feel like you're burning to death the first couple of times, but I've

1446
01:10:13,520 --> 01:10:14,520
come to really enjoy it.

1447
01:10:14,520 --> 01:10:18,520
The Finnish sauna is the hottest of all the world saunas, typically said.

1448
01:10:18,520 --> 01:10:21,520
Let's talk a little bit more about your future aspirations.

